// Source: August 2008 public exam, item 61(b).
E("es-u2-cr-q01",2,["EA12.2.1"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Two hikers are looking at a valley that was formed by a river. Describe the different ways the hikers would describe the formation of the valley if one hiker had a uniformitarian view and the other had a catastrophist view.",["Uniformitarian view: the valley was carved gradually, over millions of years, by river erosion and weathering that still operate today. Catastrophist view: the valley formed rapidly, over a short time, by a sudden catastrophic event."],"<b>Model answer:</b> The uniformitarian hiker would say the valley formed over an extremely long period of time (perhaps millions of years) through physical processes such as erosion and weathering, which have shaped Earth throughout its history and still act today. The catastrophist hiker would say the valley formed over a relatively short period of time through some sudden, catastrophic event, such as an earthquake.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: the uniformitarian explanation (gradual processes over a very long time).<br>- 1 mark: the catastrophist explanation (a sudden event over a short time).<br><br>",{});}]),["One view is gradual and one is sudden; name the process in each."]);

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
// Source: August 2006 public exam, item 61(b).
E("es-u2-cr-q02",2,["EA12.2.1","EA12.2.19"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Using the concept of uniformitarianism, predict the effect that this concept would have on future mass extinctions.",["Processes that caused past extinctions (plate tectonics, meteorite impacts) still operate today, so mass extinctions are likely to occur again in the future."],"<b>Model answer:</b> Uniformitarianism says the processes operating today also operated in the past and will continue into the future. Mass extinctions in the past were driven by processes such as plate tectonics and meteorite impacts, and because those same processes continue, mass extinctions may occur again in the future.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: identifying a process that caused past extinctions and still operates (plate tectonics, meteorite impact).<br>- 1 mark: concluding that mass extinctions may therefore happen again.<br><br>",{});}]),["Past causes, still operating, applied to the future."]);

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
// Source: Dr Dean sample final, item 77(b).
E("es-u2-cr-q03",2,["EA12.2.1","EA12.2.12"],3,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Based on the information below, answer the question that follows. 'The Earth has been covered by giant combinations of continents, called supercontinents, many times in its past, and it will be again one day in the distant future. The next predicted supercontinent ... may form when the Americas and Asia both drift northward to merge, closing off the Arctic Ocean ....' (Charles Q. Choi, Our Amazing Planet) Identify and explain a concept that scientists could use to support the idea suggested in the above quotation.",["Uniformitarianism: the processes that formed and broke up past supercontinents still operate today, so they will keep operating and can build another supercontinent."],"<b>Model answer:</b> The concept is uniformitarianism. It holds that the same geologic processes that operated in the past are still operating today and will continue to operate in the future. Earth has formed and destroyed supercontinents before through plate movement; since those same processes continue, another supercontinent can form again.<br><br><b>Marking points (3 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: identifying the concept as uniformitarianism.<br>- 1 mark: explaining the concept (past processes still operate today and will continue).<br>- 1 mark: relating it to past and future supercontinents.<br><br>",{});}]),["Which concept links Earth's past behaviour to its future?"]);

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
// Source: June 2008 public exam, item 61(a).
E("es-u2-cr-q04",2,["EA12.2.3"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Explain how superposition and cross-cutting relationships are used in relative dating.",["Superposition orders undisturbed layers (bottom oldest); a feature that cuts across rocks is younger than the rocks it cuts."],"<b>Model answer:</b> Superposition: in an undisturbed sedimentary sequence, each layer is older than the one above it, so the relative order of layers can be read from the bottom up. Cross-cutting relationships: any feature that cuts across a rock (a dyke, sill or fault) must be younger than the rock it cuts, because the rock had to exist first. Together the two principles let geologists place rock units and events in chronological order without knowing their numerical ages.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: superposition correctly explained.<br>- 1 mark: cross-cutting relationships correctly explained.<br><br>The scheme lists a third point (using the principles to arrange events chronologically); the item carries 2 marks, so two full points earn full credit.",{});}]),["One principle uses position; the other uses what cuts what."]);

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
// Source: June 2009 public exam, item 63(f).
E("es-u2-cr-q05",2,["EA12.2.3"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Angular unconformities are more commonly found in mountain-building regions of Earth than disconformities. Explain why this is so.",["Mountain building folds and tilts layers; erosion then bevels them and new flat layers are deposited on top, producing an angular unconformity. A disconformity is only an erosion surface between parallel layers, needing no tilting."],"<b>Model answer:</b> In mountain-building regions, compression during orogenesis folds and tilts the rock layers. When the tilted layers are uplifted and eroded, and deposition later resumes with flat-lying layers on top, the result is an angular unconformity. A disconformity, by contrast, is simply an erosional surface between parallel layers, so it requires no tilting and is not tied to mountain building.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: tilting or folding by compression during mountain building, followed by erosion and renewed deposition.<br>- 1 mark: the contrast with a disconformity (parallel layers, erosion surface only).<br><br>The original item supplied a figure naming the two unconformity types; the comparison is stated in words here.",{});}]),["What does mountain building do to layers before erosion acts?"]);

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
// Source: June 2008 public exam, item 63(a)(i).
E("es-u2-cr-q06",2,["EA12.2.4"],3,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Use the list of events below to answer the question that follows. A: deposition of shale. B: folding of pre-existing limestone. C: intrusion of granite. D: deposition of limestone. E: erosion of present-day surface. F: intrusion of basalt. G: deposition of sandstone. Arrange the events in chronological order from oldest to youngest.",["D, B, G, C, A, F, E"],"<b>Model answer:</b> D, B, G, C, A, F, E. The limestone (D) must be deposited before it can be folded (B). Sandstone (G) lies on the folded limestone. The granite (C) cuts the sandstone, the shale (A) is deposited over the granite, the basalt (F) cuts the shale, and the present-day erosion surface (E) truncates everything, so it is last.<br><br><b>Marking points (3 marks):</b><br>- 0.5 mark per letter placed in the correct position, to a maximum of 3 marks.<br><br>",{});}]),["The limestone exists before it can fold; the erosion surface truncates everything."]);

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
// Source: August 2008 public exam, item 63(g).
E("es-u2-cr-q07",2,["EA12.2.4"],3,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Draw a cross-section showing the following sequence of events: (i) sandstone, shale, and conglomerate are deposited in horizontal layers; (ii) the layers are folded into a syncline and an anticline; (iii) an erosion event (E) occurs; (iv) a limestone unit (L) is deposited on top of the sequence; (v) magma (M) intrudes the entire sequence but does not reach the surface; (vi) contact metamorphism (X) occurs along the sides of the intrusion. Use the legend: SS = sandstone, S = shale, C = conglomerate, LS = limestone, E = erosion, M = magma, X = contact metamorphism.",["Horizontal SS, S, C layers folded into a syncline and anticline, cut by erosion surface E, flat limestone on top, intrusion M crossing all units without reaching the surface, X symbols along both margins of M."],"<b>Model answer:</b> Draw the three sedimentary units as horizontal layers in the order deposited, fold the whole stack into one anticline and one syncline, and truncate the folds with the erosion surface E. Lay the limestone flat across the top of E. Draw the magma body M cutting up through every unit, including the limestone, but stopping below the surface, with X marked along both contacts where the magma baked the surrounding rock.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: correct event order in the drawing (deposition, folding, erosion, limestone).<br>- 1 mark: intrusion M cutting the whole sequence without reaching the surface, with contact metamorphism X along its margins, all units labelled from the legend.<br><br>",{});}]),["Build the drawing event by event, in order."]);

