E("es-u2-cr-q13",2,["EA12.2.7"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Use the data in the table below to answer the questions that follow. Sample A: half-life 2.5 million years, fraction of parent isotope remaining 1/128. Sample B: half-life 4.3 million years, fraction of parent isotope remaining 1/16. (i) Determine the number of half-lives experienced by each sample. (ii) Which sample is older? Justify your answer.",["(i) A: 7 half-lives; B: 4 half-lives. (ii) Sample A is older: A = 7 x 2.5 = 17.5 million years; B = 4 x 4.3 = 17.2 million years."],"<b>Model answer:</b> (i) Sample A: 1/128 remaining is (1/2) to the seventh power, so 7 half-lives. Sample B: 1/16 remaining is (1/2) to the fourth power, so 4 half-lives. (ii) Sample A is older. Age of A = 7 x 2.5 million = 17.5 million years. Age of B = 4 x 4.3 million = 17.2 million years. More half-lives does not automatically mean older; the half-life length must be multiplied in.<br><br><b>Marking points (4 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: 7 half-lives for Sample A.<br>- 1 mark: 4 half-lives for Sample B.<br>- 1 mark: the two calculated ages (17.5 and 17.2 million years).<br>- 1 mark: Sample A identified as older, justified by the ages.<br><br>",{});}]),["Convert each fraction to a count of halvings, then multiply by each half-life."]);

