E("es-u2-cr-q14",2,["EA12.2.7"],2,"var",varset([function(){var q=textq("The diagram below shows the pattern of radioactive decay in a mineral sample. Use it to calculate the age of the sample. [ A = undecayed atoms, B = decayed atoms. In the diagram's key, an open circle is an undecayed atom and a solid circle is a decayed atom. The half-life for the radioactive sample is 2.3 million years. ]",["8 of 32 atoms remain undecayed, so 1/4 remains: 2 half-lives; age = 2 x 2.3 million = 4.6 million years."],"<b>Model answer:</b> Count the atoms in the grid: 8 of the 32 original atoms are still undecayed, so the fraction remaining is 8/32 = 1/4, which is 2 half-lives. Age = 2 x 2.3 million years = 4.6 million years.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: the fraction remaining (1/4) converted to 2 half-lives.<br>- 1 mark: the age, 4.6 million years.<br><br>",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u2-27-aug07-61a.png" alt="Diagram for this question" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["Count undecayed atoms out of the total first."]);

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
// Source: June 2008 public exam, item 61(b).
E("es-u2-cr-q15",2,["EA12.2.7"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Radioactive element X has a half-life of 200,000 years. Given that there was originally 256 grams of element X in a rock, how much element X would be left if the rock is 1 million years old?",["1,000,000 / 200,000 = 5 half-lives; 256 to 128 to 64 to 32 to 16 to 8 g; 8 g remains."],"<b>Model answer:</b> Number of half-lives = 1,000,000 / 200,000 = 5. Halving 256 g five times: 128, 64, 32, 16, 8. So 8 g of element X remains.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: determining that 5 half-lives have passed.<br>- 1 mark: the remaining mass, 8 g.<br><br>",{});}]),["Divide the rock's age by the half-life, then halve the mass that many times."]);

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
// Source: June 2009 public exam, item 61(a).
E("es-u2-cr-q16",2,["EA12.2.7"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Calculate the age of a mummified human if there is 6.25 percent of its original carbon-14 remaining. [Half-life of carbon-14 = 5,730 years]. Show all calculations.",["6.25% = 1/16 remaining, so 4 half-lives; age = 4 x 5,730 = 22,920 years."],"<b>Model answer:</b> Halving from 100 percent: 50, 25, 12.5, 6.25. Reaching 6.25 percent takes 4 half-lives. Age = 4 x 5,730 = 22,920 years.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: determining that 4 half-lives have passed.<br>- 1 mark: the age, 22,920 years.<br><br>",{});}]),["Halve 100 percent until you reach 6.25 percent, and count the steps."]);

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
// Source: Dr Dean sample final, item 77(a).
E("es-u2-cr-q17",2,["EA12.2.7"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("A sample was radioactively dated to be 2139 million years old. If the half-life of the radioactive isotope used is 713 million years and the rock originally contained 260 grams of parent isotope, what is the amount of parent isotope presently in the rock? (Show calculations below)",["2139 / 713 = 3 half-lives; 260 to 130 to 65 to 32.5 g; 32.5 g remains."],"<b>Model answer:</b> Number of half-lives = 2139 / 713 = 3. Halving 260 g three times: 130, 65, 32.5. So 32.5 g of the parent isotope remains.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: determining that 3 half-lives have passed (2139 divided by 713).<br>- 1 mark: the remaining mass, 32.5 g.<br><br>",{});}]),["This one runs the usual calculation in reverse: age first, then mass."]);

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
// Source: June 2009 public exam, item 61(b).
E("es-u2-cr-q18",2,["EA12.11.5"],3,"var",varset([function(){return textq("In the 1950's, scientists determined the age of Earth to be 4.5 billion years old based on uranium/lead dating of meteorites. Canadian scientists at the Geological Survey of Canada initially determined an age of 3.5 billion years old for a rock sample. This age was later found to be incorrect and the rock was actually 2.8 billion years old. Which error most likely occurred during the dating of this rock sample to cause the age to be 3.5 billion years old, and why did this error cause an overestimation of age?",["Lead from the seals of the sample containers contaminated the sample, adding extra daughter isotope; more daughter makes more half-lives appear to have passed, so the calculated age was too old."],"<b>Model answer:</b> The error was contamination by the daughter isotope: lead from the seals of the sample containers entered the sample, raising the measured amount of lead (daughter product). A higher daughter-to-parent ratio makes it appear that more half-lives have passed than actually have, so the calculated age (3.5 billion years) overestimated the true age (2.8 billion years).<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: the error (extra lead from the container seals added to the sample).<br>- 1 mark: the mechanism (added daughter isotope inflates the ratio and the calculated number of half-lives, so the age comes out too old).<br><br>",{});}]),["The date came out too old: which isotope measurement was inflated, and how?"]);

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
// Source: August 2009 public exam, item 61(b).
E("es-u2-cr-q19",2,["EA12.11.5","EA12.2.7"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Using the data below, explain which pair of isotopes could most accurately be used to date an early Precambrian rock sample. Uranium-238 and Lead-206: half-life 4.5 billion years. Uranium-235 and Lead-207: half-life 713 million years. Carbon-14 and Nitrogen-14: half-life 5,730 years.",["Uranium-235 and Lead-207: an early Precambrian rock is about 4.4 billion years old, about 6 half-lives of U-235, leaving measurable parent; C-14's half-life is far too short and U-238's is too long (less than one half-life)."],"<b>Model answer:</b> An early Precambrian rock is approximately 4.4 billion years old. The most accurate pair is uranium-235 and lead-207: dividing 4.4 billion years by the 713-million-year half-life gives about 6 half-lives, leaving a significant, measurable amount of parent isotope. Carbon-14 and nitrogen-14 would not work: the 5,730-year half-life means roughly 767,000 half-lives would have passed, leaving an insignificant amount of carbon-14 to measure. Uranium-238 and lead-206 would also be inaccurate: with a 4.5-billion-year half-life, less than one half-life has passed, so the small amount of decay carries a large error.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: choosing uranium-235 and lead-207.<br>- 1 mark: the explanation, including why at least one other pair fails (C-14 far too short, or U-238 too long for even one half-life).<br><br>Note the official answer rejects the longest half-life: less than one half-life of decay is too little to measure accurately.",{});}]),["A good pair leaves a measurable amount of parent after the rock's whole lifetime."]);

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
// Source: Authored for this bank from the curriculum guide's Labrador zircons STSE elaboration for EA12.7.3.
E("es-u2-cr-q20",2,["EA12.7.3"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Zircon crystals from Labrador are among the oldest materials dated on Earth. In your own words, explain how scientists use radioactive decay in such zircons to establish an absolute age, using appropriate scientific language and conventions.",["Uranium-238 in the zircon decays to lead-206 at a constant half-life of 4.5 billion years; measuring the parent-to-daughter ratio gives the number of half-lives since crystallization, and multiplying gives the age in years."],"<b>Model answer:</b> When a zircon crystallizes, it incorporates uranium but excludes lead, so its clock starts at zero daughter product. The parent isotope uranium-238 then decays to the daughter isotope lead-206 at a constant rate, with a half-life of 4.5 billion years. Measuring the parent-to-daughter ratio in the zircon gives the number of half-lives that have passed since crystallization; multiplying that number by 4.5 billion years gives the absolute age of the zircon.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: decay at a constant, known half-life, with parent and daughter isotopes named correctly.<br>- 1 mark: the ratio converted to a number of half-lives and then to an age stated with units.",{});}]),["Name the parent, the daughter, the half-life, and the step from ratio to age."]);

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
// Source: August 2007 public exam, item 64(a).
E("es-u2-cr-q21",2,["EA12.2.10"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Give reasons why an earthworm is not likely to be preserved as a fossil in the fossil record.",["No hard body parts; soft tissue decays quickly; lives in soil exposed to bacteria and predators."],"<b>Model answer:</b> An earthworm has no hard body parts such as shell or bone, so there is nothing resistant to preserve. It lives in soil, where it is exposed to predators and decay organisms, and its soft body decomposes quickly after death, long before burial could protect it.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: no hard body parts.<br>- 1 mark: rapid decay of the soft body before burial (soil habitat, predation or decomposition).<br><br>",{});}]),["Run the fossilization conditions and check the earthworm against each."]);

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
// Source: June 2009 public exam, item 64(a).
E("es-u2-cr-q22",2,["EA12.2.10"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Remains of dinosaurs have been found in the Badlands of Alberta. (i) What type of fossils most likely exist in the rock to show proof that dinosaurs once lived in that area? Explain how this type of fossil formed. (ii) Jellyfish have been found in the fossil record. What is one main condition that is necessary for the Jellyfish to become fossilized?",["(i) Trace fossils: indirect evidence such as footprints, gastroliths and coprolites, formed when activity marks or stomach stones were buried and preserved. (ii) Rapid burial."],"<b>Model answer:</b> (i) Trace fossils. They are indirect evidence of the dinosaurs rather than body parts: footprints left in sediment that was later buried and lithified, gastroliths (polished stones swallowed to grind food), and coprolites (fossilized dung) all record dinosaur activity in the area. (ii) A jellyfish is soft-bodied and decays within days, so the one essential condition is rapid burial, sealing the body away from scavengers and decay before it breaks down.<br><br><b>Marking points (3 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: trace fossils identified as indirect evidence.<br>- 1 mark: a valid example with how it formed (footprints, gastroliths or coprolites).<br>- 1 mark: rapid burial as the condition for the jellyfish.<br><br>",{});}]),["Part (i) asks about evidence of activity, not body parts."]);

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
// Source: August 2007 public exam, item 64(b).
E("es-u2-cr-q23",2,["EA12.2.11"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Describe how a beetle could be preserved as a fossil.",["Its hard chitin shell resists decay: it can form a mold and cast, be carbonized, or be trapped in amber."],"<b>Model answer:</b> A beetle has a hard outer shell of chitin, which resists decay long enough for fossilization. If the shell is buried and later dissolves, it leaves a mold that can fill with sediment or minerals to form a cast. Compression can carbonize the shell, leaving a thin carbon film. A beetle can also be trapped in tree resin, which hardens into amber and preserves the whole insect.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: the hard chitin shell as the feature that allows preservation.<br>- 1 mark: a correctly described process (mold and cast, carbonization, or preservation in amber).<br><br>",{});}]),["Start from the body part that makes preservation possible."]);

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
// Source: August 2008 public exam, item 64(a).
E("es-u2-cr-q24",2,["EA12.2.11"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Explain how a carbonization fossil of a leaf can form.",["The leaf is buried between sediment layers; compression and time drive off its water and gases, leaving a thin dark film of carbon that keeps the leaf's outline."],"<b>Model answer:</b> A leaf that settles into quiet water is buried between layers of fine sediment. As more sediment accumulates, pressure and time drive off the leaf's volatile compounds (its water and gases), leaving behind a thin, dark film of nearly pure carbon that preserves the leaf's shape and outline as an impression.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: burial between sediment layers and compression.<br>- 1 mark: loss of volatile compounds leaving a thin carbon film impression.<br><br>",{});}]),["What is driven off, and what is left behind?"]);

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
// Source: August 2006 public exam, item 63(d)(ii).
E("es-u2-cr-q25",2,["EA12.2.12"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Marine fossils are found high in the Himalayan Mountains. Explain how these fossils were able to form at such high elevations.",["The fossils formed in sea-floor sediments long before the mountains existed; the sediments were later squeezed and uplifted when India collided with Asia."],"<b>Model answer:</b> The marine organisms lived and were fossilized in sediments deposited in an ancient sea, long before the Himalayas existed. When the Indian plate collided with the Asian plate (a continent-continent collision), those sea-floor sedimentary rocks were squeezed, folded and uplifted thousands of metres, carrying the marine fossils to their present high elevations.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: the fossils formed in a marine environment before the mountains formed.<br>- 1 mark: uplift of the sea-floor rocks during the India-Asia collision.<br><br>This is the second part of a 4-mark item whose first part (Himalaya formation) belongs to Unit 4; the split is disclosed here, as in the build report.",{});}]),["The fossils are older than the mountains: what moved them up?"]);

