/* ----- EA12.8.8: Identify careers that relate to the study of rocks ----- */
E("es-u3-88-q01",3,["EA12.8.8"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Which career studies the origin, composition and classification of rocks in the field and laboratory?","A petrologist",["A hydrologist","A meteorologist","An astronomer"],"Petrology is the study of rocks themselves: their mineral makeup, textures and origins. A petrologist is the rocks specialist among the careers listed.",{});}]),["The career name is built from the Greek word for rock."]);
E("es-u3-88-q02",3,["EA12.8.8"],2,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("A geological survey is mapping bedrock to decide where a new quarry could supply crushed rock for road construction. Which specialist leads the rock side of that work?","A bedrock geologist who maps and tests the rock units",["A geophysicist who surveys the area with seismic instruments only","A hydrogeologist who tests the groundwater chemistry only","A soil scientist who classifies the surface soils only"],"Quarry siting depends on the identity, extent and quality of the bedrock units, which is the work of a geologist who studies and maps rocks. The other specialists contribute supporting data but do not lead the rock assessment.",{});}]),["The decision is about the rock units themselves."]);

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
E("es-u3-cr-q01",3,["EA12.3.4"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Based on the definition of a mineral, ice would be considered a mineral and coal would not. Explain why this is true.",["ice meets all five mineral criteria but coal is organic"],"<b>Model answer:</b> A mineral must (1) occur naturally, (2) be inorganic, (3) be solid, (4) have a definite chemical composition, and (5) have a definite (crystalline) molecular structure. Ice satisfies all five conditions. Coal fails the test because it is composed of carbon that originated from living, organic material.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b> 1 mark for stating the mineral criteria (or the relevant ones); 1 mark for applying them: ice meets all five, coal is organic in origin.",{});}]));

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
E("es-u3-cr-q02",3,["EA12.3.7","EA12.3.6"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Place each chemical formula in the correct mineral group: CaCO3, Fe2SiO4, Fe3O4, SiO2, Al2O3, CaMg(CO3)2. Groups: Silicate, Carbonate, Oxide.",["silicate: fe2sio4, sio2; carbonate: caco3, camg(co3)2; oxide: fe3o4, al2o3"],"<b>Model answer:</b> Silicate: Fe2SiO4, SiO2. Carbonate: CaCO3, CaMg(CO3)2. Oxide: Fe3O4, Al2O3.<br><br><b>Marking points (3 marks):</b> 0.5 marks for each correctly placed formula, to a total of 3 marks (official scheme).",{});}]));

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
E("es-u3-cr-q03",3,["EA12.3.9"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("A mineral sample was studied and the following data obtained. Using the data, determine the mineral's specific gravity. Mass of mineral sample: 129.6 g. Volume of water displaced: 18.0 cm3.",["7.2"],"<b>Model answer:</b> Specific gravity equals the mineral's density relative to water: SG = mass / volume = 129.6 g / 18.0 cm3 = 7.2. The mineral's specific gravity is 7.2 (no units).<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b> 1 mark for the correct relationship (SG = mass/volume); 1 mark for the correct value, 7.2.",{});}]));

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
E("es-u3-cr-q04",3,["EA12.3.10"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Two mineral samples that look similar are known to be quartz and calcite. Explain two ways how you can distinguish quartz from calcite.",["hardness test and acid test","quartz is harder and does not fizz in acid"],"<b>Model answer:</b> Any two of: (1) Hardness: quartz (7) is much harder than calcite (3); a steel file scratches calcite but not quartz. (2) Cleavage: calcite has perfect cleavage in three directions, while quartz has no cleavage and shows conchoidal fracture. (3) Acid test: calcite fizzes (effervesces) in dilute acid; quartz does not react. (4) Crystal form: calcite is rhombohedral, quartz is hexagonal.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b> 1 mark for each valid distinguishing test correctly explained (maximum 2 marks), following the official scheme.",{});}]));

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
E("es-u3-cr-q05",3,["EA12.3.10"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Explain why specific gravity is a better property for identifying an unknown mineral rather than lustre.",["specific gravity is constant for a mineral while many minerals share the same lustre"],"<b>Model answer:</b> Specific gravity is consistent for each mineral regardless of the size and shape of the sample or where it was found, so it can pinpoint an individual mineral (for example, pyrite at 5.2 versus chalcopyrite at 4.2). Lustre sorts minerals only into broad groups, because many different minerals share the same lustre (metallic, non-metallic, earthy, pearly, dull).<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b> 1 mark for specific gravity being a constant, measurable value for a given mineral; 1 mark for lustre being shared by many minerals, so it groups rather than identifies.",{});}]));

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
E("es-u3-cr-q06",3,["EA12.3.11"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Explain the difference between a rock and a mineral. Provide an example of each.",["a mineral is a single naturally occurring inorganic solid with definite composition and structure; a rock is a consolidated mixture of minerals"],"<b>Model answer:</b> A mineral is a single substance that is solid, inorganic, naturally occurring, and has a definite chemical composition and a definite crystalline structure (for example, quartz). A rock is a solid, consolidated mixture of one or more minerals (for example, granite, a mixture of quartz, feldspar and mica).<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b> 1 mark for the mineral definition applied; 1 mark for a rock as a mixture (aggregate) of minerals, with a valid example of each.",{});}]));

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
E("es-u3-cr-q07",3,["EA12.3.12"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Explain the difference between igneous rocks that are extrusive and those that are intrusive. Provide an example of each.",["extrusive rocks cool quickly at the surface and are fine grained; intrusive rocks cool slowly underground and are coarse grained"],"<b>Model answer:</b> Extrusive igneous rocks form from lava cooling quickly at the surface, so they are fine grained (aphanitic), for example rhyolite, andesite or basalt. Intrusive igneous rocks form from magma cooling slowly beneath the surface, so they are coarse grained (phaneritic), for example granite, diorite or gabbro. The most noticeable difference is texture, which records the cooling rate.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b> 1 mark for the extrusive description with an example; 1 mark for the intrusive description with an example.",{});}]));

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
E("es-u3-cr-q08",3,["EA12.3.16"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Describe two things that can be determined by examining the crystal size of minerals in igneous rocks.",["the environment of cooling (intrusive or extrusive) and the cooling rate"],"<b>Model answer:</b> (1) The environment in which the molten rock cooled: large crystals indicate an intrusive (plutonic) environment underground, and small crystals an extrusive (volcanic) environment at the surface. (2) The cooling rate or history of the melt: large crystals record slow cooling, and small crystals record fast cooling.<br><br><b>Marking points (3 marks):</b> 1.5 marks for the environment determination with the crystal size matched to each environment; 1.5 marks for the cooling rate determination with the crystal size matched to each rate (official scheme).",{});}]));

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
E("es-u3-cr-q09",3,["EA12.3.18"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Briefly describe how a geologist would use texture and mineral composition to determine the type of igneous rock.",["texture records the cooling environment and rate; composition groups the rock as felsic, intermediate, mafic or ultramafic"],"<b>Model answer:</b> Texture (the size, shape and arrangement of the crystals) records the cooling environment and rate: fast cooling gives a glassy or fine (aphanitic) texture, and slow cooling gives a coarse (phaneritic) texture. Mineral composition records the melt's chemistry: the proportions of quartz, feldspars, pyroxene, amphibole and olivine place the rock in the felsic, intermediate, mafic or ultramafic family. Texture and composition together name the rock (for example, coarse grained and felsic is granite).<br><br><b>Marking points (3 marks):</b> 1.5 marks for texture correctly explained and tied to cooling; 1.5 marks for composition correctly explained and tied to rock families.",{});}]));

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
E("es-u3-cr-q10",3,["EA12.3.19"],3,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Describe the formation of kimberlite and explain its relationship with diamond deposits.",["kimberlite is an ultramafic magma from deep in the mantle that erupts rapidly through carrot-shaped pipes, carrying diamonds formed at depth to the surface"],"<b>Model answer:</b> Kimberlite is an ultramafic magma that originates deep in the mantle, at depths where the pressure is high enough for diamond, rather than graphite, to be the stable form of carbon. Gas-charged kimberlite magma rises extremely rapidly and erupts through narrow, carrot-shaped pipes. Diamond crystals that formed at depth are picked up and carried to the surface as foreign fragments (xenocrysts) before they can convert to graphite, which is why kimberlite pipes are mined as the world's primary source of natural diamonds.<br><br><b>Marking points (3 marks):</b> 1 mark for the deep mantle (ultramafic) origin; 1 mark for rapid ascent through pipes; 1 mark for diamonds forming at depth and being carried up as passengers, not crystallizing from the kimberlite itself.",{});}]));

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
E("es-u3-cr-q11",3,["EA12.3.20"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("In relation to the rock cycle, explain two processes responsible for the formation of sedimentary rocks.",["compaction and cementation"],"<b>Model answer:</b> Sediment becomes rock by lithification (diagenesis), in two stages. (1) Compaction: as more sediment piles on top, its weight squeezes the grains closer together, reducing pore space and driving out water. (2) Cementation: mineral matter such as silica or calcite, carried in the remaining pore water, precipitates in the spaces between grains and binds them into solid rock.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b> 1 mark for each process correctly explained (compaction, cementation), following the official scheme.",{});}]));

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
E("es-u3-cr-q12",3,["EA12.3.29"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Give two reasons why swamps provide a suitable environment for coal formation.",["abundant organic material and an oxygen-deficient environment"],"<b>Model answer:</b> Swamps supply (1) abundant organic material from lush plant growth and (2) a low-oxygen (anaerobic) environment in stagnant water, with a lack of decay bacteria, so plant matter accumulates instead of rotting away. Burial then adds the moisture, temperature and compaction conditions that convert the accumulation toward coal.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b> 1 mark for any two of: low-oxygen environment, abundant organic material, lack of decay bacteria (official scheme).",{});}]));

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
E("es-u3-cr-q13",3,["EA12.3.31"],3,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Describe two sedimentary features that can be used to determine if a sedimentary bed has been inverted (i.e., overturned).",["graded bedding with coarse grains at the top, or mud cracks opening downward"],"<b>Model answer:</b> Any two of: (1) Graded bedding: in an overturned bed the coarser sediment is at the top and the finer at the bottom, the reverse of normal settling. (2) Mud cracks: the V-shaped cracks open downward, or the crack edges curl downward, instead of opening upward. (3) Fossils: shells are found with their open (concave) side up. (4) Cross bedding or ripple marks: the truncated and curved surfaces appear reversed from their normal orientation.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b> 1 mark for each feature correctly described in its inverted form (maximum 2 marks), following the official scheme.",{});}]));

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
E("es-u3-cr-q14",3,["EA12.3.31"],3,"var",varset([function(){return textq("With reference to the rock cycle, describe two reasons that account for over 40 percent of the sedimentary rocks on Earth's surface being younger than 100 million years.",["older sedimentary rocks have had more time to be weathered and eroded away, or to be recycled by melting and subduction"],"<b>Model answer:</b> Any two of: (1) Time for destruction: older sedimentary rocks have been exposed to weathering and erosion for longer, so a smaller share of them survives. (2) Recycling: with time, sedimentary rocks are increasingly likely to have been buried, melted back into magma (returning as igneous rock), metamorphosed, or subducted, removing them from the sedimentary record. Younger rocks have simply had less opportunity to be destroyed or recycled.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b> 1 mark for each valid rock-cycle reason (maximum 2 marks), following the sample answer key.",{});}]));

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
E("es-u3-cr-q15",3,["EA12.3.32"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Explain how sedimentary rocks may eventually form metamorphic rocks.",["sedimentary rocks buried and exposed to heat, pressure and chemically active fluids recrystallize into metamorphic rocks"],"<b>Model answer:</b> Sedimentary rocks become metamorphic rocks when burial or tectonics exposes them to high heat, high pressure, or chemically active fluids. Heat bakes the rock and can grow new minerals; pressure compacts and aligns minerals (producing foliation); fluids change the chemistry. The rock recrystallizes in the solid state: for example, shale becomes slate, then phyllite, schist and gneiss as the grade increases.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b> 1 mark for naming the metamorphic conditions (heat, pressure, fluids); 1 mark for explaining the change as recrystallization in the solid state, ideally with an example.",{});}]));

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
E("es-u3-cr-q16",3,["EA12.3.33"],3,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Explain the sequence of changes that will occur in shale as it is buried deeper and deeper within the folded mountains at a continent-to-continent plate collision.",["shale becomes slate, then phyllite, then schist, then gneiss as grade increases"],"<b>Model answer:</b> As shale is buried deeper, heat and pressure increase and the grade of metamorphism rises in a predictable sequence. Low grade: shale recrystallizes into slate, a hard rock that splits along flat cleavage. Increasing grade: slate becomes phyllite, with a slight sheen from growing mica. Higher grade: phyllite becomes schist, with visible mica crystals and strong foliation. Highest grade: schist becomes gneiss, with minerals segregated into light and dark bands.<br><br><b>Marking points (3 marks):</b> 1 mark for the driving conditions (rising heat and pressure with burial); 2 marks for the correct sequence (slate, phyllite, schist, gneiss) with at least two correct descriptions of the changes.",{});}]));

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
E("es-u3-cr-q17",3,["EA12.3.34","EA12.3.29"],3,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Why is anthracite often found in association with slate rather than shale or sandstone?",["anthracite and slate are both metamorphic products formed under similar heat and pressure, while shale and sandstone are unmetamorphosed sedimentary rocks"],"<b>Model answer:</b> Anthracite is the highest grade of coal and slate is a low-grade metamorphic rock; both are metamorphic in origin and form under similar conditions of heat and pressure, so they occur together in deformed, metamorphosed terrain. Shale and sandstone are sedimentary rocks that have not undergone those conditions.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b> 1 mark for recognizing anthracite and slate as metamorphic products of similar conditions; 1 mark for recognizing shale and sandstone as sedimentary (official scheme).",{});}]));

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
E("es-u3-cr-q18",3,["EA12.3.35"],3,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Explain the difference between regional metamorphism and contact metamorphism within a mountain environment. Include a labelled diagram with your answer.",["regional metamorphism changes rock over a large area under heat and directed pressure; contact metamorphism bakes rock locally around an intrusion with heat alone"],"<b>Model answer:</b> Regional metamorphism: both heat and directed pressure act together over a large area, as in the deep interior of a mountain belt, producing foliated rocks such as schist and gneiss. Contact metamorphism: heat from a nearby magma body alone bakes the surrounding rock over a small area (an aureole), producing nonfoliated rocks such as hornfels or marble. A labelled diagram should show a magma body with a narrow baked zone around it, set within a much larger region of folded, foliated rock.<br><br><b>Marking points (3 marks):</b> 1 mark for regional (heat plus pressure, large area); 1 mark for contact (heat only, small area around an intrusion); 1 mark for a labelled diagram showing both settings.",{});}]));

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
E("es-u3-cr-q19",3,["EA12.3.35"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Explain the relationship between subduction and the formation of both types of metamorphic rocks.",["subduction supplies the heat and pressure for regional metamorphism and the magma whose heat causes contact metamorphism"],"<b>Model answer:</b> At a subduction zone, the sinking plate carries rock to depths of high heat and pressure, and the compression of the collision drives regional metamorphism over a wide area. The subducting plate also generates magma, which rises into the crust; rock baked around those magma bodies undergoes contact metamorphism. Subduction therefore drives both types.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b> 1 mark for subduction producing the heat and pressure of regional metamorphism; 1 mark for subduction-generated magma producing contact metamorphism (sample answer key).",{});}]));

