/* ----- EA12.9.1: Outline the evidence used to support the theory of continental drift ----- */
// Source: Dr Dean sample final, item 79(c).
E("es-u4-cr-q01",4,["EA12.9.1"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("State two pieces of evidence that were used to support the theory of continental drift.",["Any two of: the jigsaw fit of the continents; fossil correlation between continents; folded mountain ranges and shield rocks lining up when the continents are reassembled; paleoclimatic evidence such as coal deposits and glacial deposits."],"<b>Model answer:</b> Any two of the following: (1) The continents fit together like pieces of a jigsaw puzzle, such as South America and Africa. (2) Identical fossils are found on continents now separated by oceans. (3) Folded mountain ranges and ancient shield rocks line up across the Atlantic when the continents are put back together. (4) Paleoclimatic evidence: coal deposits (formed in warm, wet climates) and glacial deposits are found in places whose present climates do not match them.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark for each of two valid pieces of evidence.<br><br>",{});}]),["Drift was argued from fit and from matching records, not from a mechanism."]);
// Source: August 2009 public exam, item 64(a).
E("es-u4-cr-q02",4,["EA12.9.1"],3,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Contrast the theory of continental drift with the theory of plate tectonics.",["Continental drift was supported by the fit of the continents, matching rocks and fossils, and paleoclimate evidence, but proposed no mechanism. Plate tectonics explains the movement: convection in the asthenosphere drives the plates, supported by seafloor spreading, magnetic reversals, ocean floor age, hotspots, Wadati-Benioff zones and polar wandering."],"<b>Model answer:</b> Continental drift held that the continents moved, based on evidence such as the fit of the continents, matching rock types and fossils, and paleoclimatic evidence (coal and glacial deposits), but it offered no mechanism for the movement. Plate tectonics supplies the mechanism and much more evidence: convection currents in the asthenosphere carry the plates, and the theory is supported by seafloor spreading, magnetic reversals, the age of the ocean floor, hotspots, Wadati-Benioff zones and polar wandering.<br><br><b>Marking points (3 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: the evidence base of continental drift (fit, rocks, fossils, paleoclimate).<br>- 1 mark: noting that drift proposed no mechanism.<br>- 1 mark: the plate tectonic mechanism and its added evidence (convection, seafloor spreading, magnetic reversals, ocean floor age, hotspots, Wadati-Benioff zones, polar wandering).<br><br>",{});}]),["Same moving continents; what did the newer theory add?"]);

/* ----- EA12.9.3: Explain the roles of Wegener and Holmes ----- */
// Source: August 2007 public exam, item 63(e).
E("es-u4-cr-q03",4,["EA12.9.3"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Arthur Holmes proposed that convection currents in the mantle could move the continents. Explain how convection currents could do this.",["Mantle material heated from below rises and spreads out beneath the crust, dragging the plates with it; where currents descend at subduction zones the plates are pulled down and recycled."],"<b>Model answer:</b> In Holmes's model, convection currents circulate in the asthenosphere. Hot material rises beneath the plates and spreads sideways, carrying the plates (and the continents embedded in them) along. Where the currents sink, at subduction zones, plate material is pulled down and recycled into the mantle.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: convection in the asthenosphere carries the plates as the currents rise and spread.<br>- 1 mark: material sinks and is recycled at subduction zones.<br><br>",{});}]),["Holmes supplied the engine Wegener lacked."]);

/* ----- EA12.9.6: Science and technology in daily life ----- */
// Source: June 2008 public exam, item 64(c).
E("es-u4-cr-q04",4,["EA12.9.6"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Describe two ways in which modern science and technology have improved our understanding of Earth's moving plates.",["Any two of: GPS measures plate motion accurately; satellite imagery and computer mapping produce better geologic and ocean-floor maps; monitoring technology gives early warning of tsunamis, earthquakes and volcanic eruptions."],"<b>Model answer:</b> Any two of the following: (1) Global positioning systems (GPS) measure the movement of plates directly and accurately. (2) Satellite imagery and computer mapping produce far better maps of the land surface and the ocean floor, including its magnetic stripes and ridges. (3) Monitoring networks give early warning of tsunamis, earthquakes and volcanic eruptions.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark for each of two valid technology applications.<br><br>",{});}]),["Think measurement: GPS, satellites, warning systems."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.1: Divergent plate boundaries ----- */
// Source: June 2006 public exam, item 63(c).
E("es-u4-cr-q05",4,["EA12.4.1"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("The diagram in the June 2006 exam showed the boundary between the Australian plate and the Antarctic plate, where the plates are spreading apart. Suppose this boundary were reversed, so the two plates moved toward each other instead. Describe two changes that would result.",["Any two of: the boundary becomes a convergent boundary; a trench forms; one plate subducts beneath the other; volcanic island arcs form; an accretionary wedge builds up; earthquakes occur; folded mountains form; compressional forces act on the crust."],"<b>Model answer:</b> Reversing the motion makes the boundary convergent. Any two of the following changes: a deep-ocean trench forms where the plates meet; one plate subducts beneath the other; volcanic island arcs develop above the subduction zone; an accretionary wedge of scraped-off sediment builds up; earthquakes occur along the boundary; folded mountains form; and the crust is acted on by compressional forces rather than tensional forces. The changes must be described or related to the boundary, not merely listed.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark for each of two correctly described changes.<br><br>",{});}]),["Spreading reversed is collision: name what collision builds."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.2: Convergent plate boundaries ----- */
// Source: August 2006 public exam, item 64(a).
E("es-u4-cr-q06",4,["EA12.4.2"],3,"var",varset([function(){return textq("A newly discovered planet has volcanic chains, metamorphic rocks, rhyolite and granite, and extensive reverse faulting. Explain why this planet most likely has a convergent (subduction) plate boundary.",["The features point to compression and subduction: compressional forces, plate collision, subduction, trench formation, volcanic eruptions, new rock types forming, and melting of plates and sediment."],"<b>Model answer:</b> Each feature is a product of convergence. Reverse faulting requires compressional forces, which occur where plates collide. Subduction of one plate beneath the other forms trenches and melts plate material and sediment, producing volcanic chains and new igneous rocks such as rhyolite and granite. The heat and pressure of collision also produce metamorphic rocks. Together, the features indicate a convergent subduction environment.<br><br><b>Marking points (3 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark for each of three correctly explained convergent features (compressional forces, plate collision, subduction, trench formation, volcanic eruptions, new rock types, melting of plates or sediment).<br><br>",{});}]),["Work feature by feature: what force and process makes each one?"]);
// Source: August 2008 public exam, item 63(f).
E("es-u4-cr-q07",4,["EA12.4.2"],3,"var",varset([function(){var q=textq("Use the diagram below to answer the following. (i) Describe the pattern of earthquake foci and volcanic activity across the section from west to east. (ii) Name the type of plate collision shown.",["(i) A trench lies at the west; earthquake foci deepen from shallow in the west to intermediate and deep toward the east along the descending slab; volcanoes sit on the overriding plate above where the slab reaches melting depth. (ii) An ocean-continent plate collision, with the oceanic plate subducting beneath the continental plate."],"<b>Model answer:</b> (i) At the west is an ocean trench where the oceanic plate begins to descend. Earthquake foci are shallow near the trench and become progressively deeper, intermediate then deep, toward the east, tracing the slab as it sinks. Volcanoes occur on the overriding continental plate, above the depth at which the descending slab begins to melt. (ii) This is an ocean-continent plate collision: the denser oceanic plate subducts beneath the continental plate.<br><br><b>Marking points (3 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: the west-to-east pattern of foci (shallow, then intermediate, then deep along the slab).<br>- 1 mark: volcanoes positioned above the melting depth of the slab.<br>- 1 mark: naming the collision as ocean-continent, oceanic plate subducting.<br><br>",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-42-aug08-63f.png" alt="Diagram for this question" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["Follow the slab down: the foci trace it."]);
// Source: June 2006 public exam, item 64(a).
E("es-u4-cr-q08",4,["EA12.4.2","EA12.4.21"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Describe one similarity and one difference between the rocks formed at convergent plate boundaries and the rocks formed at transform plate boundaries.",["Similarity: metamorphic rocks form at both boundary types. Difference: igneous rocks form at convergent boundaries but not at transform boundaries."],"<b>Model answer:</b> Similarity: metamorphic rocks form at both convergent and transform boundaries, because both subject rock to great pressure (and, at convergent boundaries, heat). Difference: igneous rocks form at convergent boundaries, where subduction and melting produce magma, but igneous rocks do not form at transform boundaries, where plates slide past each other without subduction or melting.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: the similarity (metamorphic rocks at both).<br>- 1 mark: the difference (igneous rocks at convergent boundaries only).<br><br>",{});}]),["One rock family is shared; one needs melting."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.3: Transform boundaries and rift valleys ----- */
// Source: June 2009 public exam, item 63(c).
E("es-u4-cr-q09",4,["EA12.4.3","EA12.9.3"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("The diagram in the June 2009 exam showed two continents separating at a mid-ocean ridge. (i) Describe the cross-section. (ii) Explain what causes the continents to move apart. (iii) Explain how new crust is created at the boundary.",["(i) Oceanic crust with a mid-oceanic ridge between the separating continents. (ii) Convection currents in the asthenosphere carry the plates apart. (iii) Mantle material upwells at the divergent ridge and solidifies as new crust: seafloor spreading."],"<b>Model answer:</b> (i) The cross-section shows oceanic crust with a mid-oceanic ridge between the two continents. (ii) Convection currents in the asthenosphere move the plates, and the continents riding on them, apart. (iii) At the divergent boundary, hot mantle material upwells to fill the gap, cools and solidifies as new oceanic crust; this is seafloor spreading.<br><br><b>Marking points (3 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: describing the cross-section (oceanic crust, 0.5; mid-oceanic ridge, 0.5).<br>- 1 mark: convection currents in the asthenosphere move the continents.<br>- 1 mark: upwelling at the ridge creates new crust (seafloor spreading).<br><br>",{});}]),["Name the ridge, the engine, and the new-crust process."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.5: Geology of Newfoundland and Labrador ----- */
// Source: June 2007 public exam, item 64(b).
E("es-u4-cr-q10",4,["EA12.4.5"],3,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Explain the origin of the three geologic zones of Newfoundland in terms of plate tectonics.",["The western zone is part of the ancient North American continent; the central zone is made of ocean-floor volcanic rocks from the Iapetus Ocean; the eastern zone is part of the ancient African continent. The zones were joined when the African plate collided with the North American plate, and part of the African plate was left behind when Pangaea broke apart."],"<b>Model answer:</b> Newfoundland's three zones record the opening and closing of the Iapetus Ocean. The western zone was part of the ancient North American plate. The central zone consists of ocean-floor volcanic rocks formed in the Iapetus Ocean. The eastern zone was part of the ancient African plate. As the plates converged, Africa collided with North America and the zones were squeezed together; when Pangaea later broke apart, a piece of the African plate remained attached, forming eastern Newfoundland.<br><br><b>Marking points (3 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: the African plate collided with the North American plate.<br>- 1 mark: ocean-floor volcanic rocks form the central region.<br>- 1 mark: the three regions are related to plate tectonics, with part of the African plate left behind when Pangaea split.<br><br>",{});}]),["West is North America, east is Africa; what lies between?"]);
// Source: June 2009 public exam, item 64(b).
E("es-u4-cr-q11",4,["EA12.4.5"],2,"var",varset([function(){var q=textq("Using the map of Newfoundland below, describe the plate tectonic origin of each of the island's three geologic zones.",["Western (Humber) Zone: ancient Laurentia, the North American continent. Central (Dunnage and Gander) Zones: crust of the Iapetus Ocean, including island arc volcanoes, with metamorphism from the collision. Eastern (Avalon) Zone: ancient Gondwanaland, the African continent."],"<b>Model answer:</b> From west to east: the Western (Humber) Zone is the margin of ancient Laurentia, the North American continent. The Central (Dunnage and Gander) Zones are remnants of the Iapetus Ocean: its crust, island arc volcanoes and sediments, metamorphosed as the ocean closed. The Eastern (Avalon) Zone is a fragment of ancient Gondwanaland, the African side of the collision, left attached to North America when the Atlantic opened.<br><br><b>Marking points (3 marks):</b><br>- 1.5 marks: correctly identifying the three zones west to east.<br>- 1.5 marks: correctly giving the origin of each zone.<br><br>",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-45-jun09-64b.png" alt="Diagram for this question" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["Read the island west to east: two continents and an ocean between."]);
// Source: August 2009 public exam, item 64(b).
E("es-u4-cr-q12",4,["EA12.4.5"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Explain how the three geologic zones of Newfoundland were formed.",["The Western Zone formed from the North American plate and the Eastern Zone from the African plate when the two collided; the Central Zone formed from the uplifted floor of the Iapetus Ocean and associated volcanism; the zones remained together as Pangaea broke apart."],"<b>Model answer:</b> The zones formed as the Iapetus Ocean closed. The Western Zone is the edge of the North American plate and the Eastern Zone is a piece of the African plate; the two continents collided, and the Central Zone formed from the uplifted floor of the Iapetus Ocean and its volcanic rocks caught between them. When Pangaea later broke apart, the three zones stayed together as part of North America.<br><br><b>Marking points (3 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: the collision of the North American (Western) and African (Eastern) plates.<br>- 1 mark: the Central Zone from the uplifted Iapetus ocean floor and volcanism.<br>- 1 mark: the zones remained together as Pangaea broke apart.<br><br>",{});}]),["An ocean closed, two continents met, and the seam stayed."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.11: Faulting ----- */
// Source: June 2007 public exam, item 63(d).
E("es-u4-cr-q13",4,["EA12.4.11"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("For each of a normal fault, a reverse fault and a transform fault, state the direction of the forces that produce it and the relative motion of the rock on either side.",["Normal: tensional forces pull the crust apart and the hanging wall moves down. Reverse: compressional forces push the crust together and the hanging wall moves up. Transform: shear forces slide the blocks horizontally past each other."],"<b>Model answer:</b> Normal fault: tensional forces pull the blocks apart, and the hanging wall moves down relative to the footwall. Reverse fault: compressional forces push the blocks together, and the hanging wall moves up relative to the footwall. Transform fault: shear forces act horizontally, and the blocks slide sideways past each other with no vertical motion.<br><br><b>Marking points (3 marks):</b><br>- 0.5 marks per fault for the correct force direction (1.5 total).<br>- 0.5 marks per fault for the correct relative motion (1.5 total).<br><br>",{});}]),["Three faults: pull apart, push together, slide past."]);
// Source: August 2007 public exam, item 63(g).
E("es-u4-cr-q14",4,["EA12.4.11"],3,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Reverse faults and thrust faults both form at convergent plate boundaries. Describe each fault type and explain how they differ.",["Both are compressional faults in which the hanging wall moves up. A reverse fault has a steep fault plane; a thrust fault is a reverse fault with a low-angle (nearly horizontal) fault plane."],"<b>Model answer:</b> At a convergent boundary, compressional forces squeeze the crust until it fractures. In both reverse and thrust faults the hanging wall is pushed up over the footwall. They differ in the angle of the fault plane: a reverse fault has a steep angle, while a thrust fault is a low-angle reverse fault, with a nearly horizontal plane along which the hanging wall is pushed a long distance over the footwall.<br><br><b>Marking points (4 marks):</b><br>- 2 marks: the reverse fault (compression fractures the plate; hanging wall moves up; steep angle).<br>- 2 marks: the thrust fault (compression fractures the plate; hanging wall moves up; low angle).<br><br>",{});}]),["Same motion, different angle."]);

// Source: Dr Dean sample final, item 79(a).
E("es-u4-cr-q15",4,["EA12.4.11"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("The diagram in the sample final showed a low-angle fault with arrows indicating compression, the hanging wall pushed up and over the footwall. Name this type of fault and describe the boundary conditions under which it forms.",["A thrust fault: a low-angle reverse fault in which the hanging wall moves up relative to the footwall. Thrust faults form at convergent plate boundaries under compressional forces."],"<b>Model answer:</b> The fault is a thrust fault, a reverse fault with a low angle of dip. The hanging wall has been pushed up and over the footwall along the nearly horizontal fault plane. Thrust faults form at convergent plate boundaries, where compressional forces squeeze the crust.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: describing the thrust fault (low-angle reverse fault; hanging wall up, footwall down; compression arrows).<br>- 1 mark: thrust faults occur at convergent boundaries under compressional forces.<br><br>",{});}]),["Low angle plus hanging wall up is the signature."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.14: Earthquake locations and depths ----- */
// Source: August 2006 public exam, item 63(e).
E("es-u4-cr-q16",4,["EA12.4.14"],2,"var",varset([function(){var q=textq("The map below shows two regions of Canada labelled A and B. Which region is most likely, and which is least likely, to experience an earthquake? Explain your choices.",["Most likely B (western Canada): it lies on the active Pacific plate margin, where the Pacific plate is subducting beneath the North American plate under compressional forces. Least likely A (eastern Canada): it lies in the interior of the North American plate, on a passive margin far from active plate boundaries."],"<b>Model answer:</b> Region B, on Canada's west coast, is most likely to experience earthquakes: it sits on the active Pacific plate margin, where the Pacific plate is subducting beneath North America and compressional forces build up along the boundary. Region A, in eastern Canada, is least likely: it lies within the interior of the North American plate, on a passive margin far from any active plate boundary, so few earthquakes occur.<br><br><b>Marking points (3 marks):</b><br>- 1.5 marks: B most likely, with the active-margin subduction explanation.<br>- 1.5 marks: A least likely, with the plate-interior passive-margin explanation.<br><br>",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-414-aug06-63e.png" alt="Diagram for this question" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["Earthquakes follow plate margins; check which region sits on one."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.15: Seismic waves ----- */
// Source: June 2006 public exam, item 63(i).
E("es-u4-cr-q17",4,["EA12.4.15"],3,"var",varset([function(){var q=textq("The diagram below shows seismic waves from an earthquake reaching stations A, B, C, D and E around Earth. Explain which waves arrive at each station and why.",["A and B receive both P and S waves through the solid crust and mantle. C is in the shadow zone: S waves are stopped and P waves refracted by the liquid outer core. D and E receive only P waves, which pass through the outer core and the solid inner core."],"<b>Model answer:</b> Stations A and B receive both P waves and S waves, because their paths pass only through the solid crust and mantle. Station C lies in the shadow zone: S waves are terminated because they cannot travel through the liquid outer core, and P waves are refracted (bent) by the outer core away from this region. Stations D and E receive only P waves, which can travel through the liquid outer core and the solid inner core.<br><br><b>Marking points (3 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: A and B receive P and S (paths through solid crust and mantle only).<br>- 1 mark: C is in the shadow zone (S terminated, P refracted by the liquid outer core).<br>- 1 mark: only P waves reach D and E, through the outer and inner core.<br><br>",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-415-jun06-63i.png" alt="Diagram for this question" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["Track each path: does it cross the liquid outer core?"]);

/* ----- EA12.4.18: Locating an epicentre ----- */
// Source: June 2007 public exam, item 63(f).
E("es-u4-cr-q18",4,["EA12.4.18"],3,"var",varset([function(){var q=textq("Use the seismogram and the travel-time graph below. (i) At what time did the P waves arrive at the station? (ii) What is the time difference between the P and S wave arrivals? (iii) How far is the station from the epicentre? (iv) A second station is 3500 km from the epicentre and its P waves arrived at 10:05. At approximately what time did its S waves arrive?",["(i) P waves arrived 2 minutes after the earthquake. (ii) The difference is 4 minutes (6 - 2). (iii) About 2250 to 2750 km. (iv) About 10:11."],"<b>Model answer:</b> (i) On the seismogram, the first disturbance (P waves) arrives at 2 minutes. (ii) The S waves arrive at 6 minutes, so the arrival time difference is 6 - 2 = 4 minutes. (iii) On the travel-time graph, a 4-minute difference between the P and S curves corresponds to a distance of about 2250 to 2750 km. (iv) At 3500 km the S-P difference is about 6 minutes, so with P arriving at 10:05 the S waves arrive at about 10:11.<br><br><b>Marking points (4 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: P arrival at 2 minutes, read from the seismogram.<br>- 1 mark: the arrival difference of 4 minutes.<br>- 1 mark: the distance, 2250 to 2750 km, from the travel-time graph.<br>- 1 mark: S arrival at about 10:11 for the 3500 km station.<br><br>",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-418-jun07-63f.png" alt="Diagram for this question" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["Read arrivals off the seismogram, then convert the gap with the graph."]);
// Source: June 2008 public exam, item 63(g).
E("es-u4-cr-q19",4,["EA12.4.18"],3,"var",varset([function(){var q=textq("Use the travel-time graph below. (i) What is the time difference between the P and S wave arrivals at a station 3000 km from the epicentre? (ii) Why does this difference exist? (iii) At a station 750 km away, the P waves arrived at 8:42:00 a.m. At what time did the S waves arrive?",["(i) 4.5 minutes. (ii) P waves travel faster than S waves. (iii) 8:43:30 a.m."],"<b>Model answer:</b> (i) At 3000 km, the travel-time graph gives an S-P arrival difference of 4.5 minutes. (ii) The difference exists because P waves travel faster than S waves through the same material, so the gap between them grows with distance. (iii) At 750 km the difference is 1.5 minutes, so the S waves arrived at 8:42:00 + 1:30 = 8:43:30 a.m.<br><br><b>Marking points (4 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: the 4.5-minute difference at 3000 km.<br>- 1 mark: P waves travel faster than S waves.<br>- 2 marks: the S arrival time of 8:43:30 a.m. for the 750 km station.<br><br>",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-418-jun08-63g.png" alt="Diagram for this question" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["The graph turns an arrival gap into a distance."]);
// Source: August 2006 public exam, item 63(f).
E("es-u4-cr-q20",4,["EA12.4.18"],2,"var",varset([function(){var q=textq("Station B recorded S waves arriving 11.5 minutes after an earthquake, and the station is 3500 km from the epicentre. Use the travel-time graph below to determine the P wave arrival time and the time difference between the P and S arrivals at Station B.",["P waves arrived at 6.25 minutes; the time difference is 11.5 - 6.25 = 5.25 minutes."],"<b>Model answer:</b> At a distance of 3500 km, the travel-time graph gives a P wave travel time of 6.25 minutes, so the P waves arrived 6.25 minutes after the earthquake. The S waves arrived at 11.5 minutes, so the time difference between the arrivals is 11.5 - 6.25 = 5.25 minutes.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: the P arrival time of 6.25 minutes from the graph.<br>- 1 mark: the arrival difference of 5.25 minutes.<br><br>",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-418-aug06-63f.png" alt="Diagram for this question" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["Distance first, then read the P curve at that distance."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.19: Volcanoes ----- */
// Source: August 2007 public exam, item 63(h).
E("es-u4-cr-q21",4,["EA12.4.19"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("A volcano's deposits alternate between slow-moving lava flows and explosive falls of ash and pyroclastic material, with rare catastrophic eruptions that blast away part of the cone. Name the type of volcano and describe how it forms.",["A composite (strato) volcano, built of alternating layers of lava and pyroclastic material at a convergent boundary, where subduction supplies magma of intermediate composition."],"<b>Model answer:</b> The volcano is a composite volcano (stratovolcano). It forms at a convergent plate boundary, where subduction generates magma of intermediate composition. Eruptions alternate: quieter lava flows spread over the cone, then explosive eruptions deposit layers of ash and pyroclastic material. The alternating layers build the tall, steep-sided cone, and occasional catastrophic eruptions can blast away part of it.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: naming the composite (strato) volcano.<br>- 1 mark: describing its formation (alternating lava flows and pyroclastic deposits at a convergent boundary).<br><br>",{});}]),["Alternating layers are the fingerprint of one volcano type."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.23: Hotspots ----- */
// Source: June 2007 public exam, item 63(e).
E("es-u4-cr-q22",4,["EA12.4.23"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Explain how hot spots provide evidence that supports the theory of plate tectonics.",["A stationary hotspot produces a chain of volcanic islands as the plate moves over it; the chain shows the direction of plate motion, and island age increases with distance from the hotspot."],"<b>Model answer:</b> A hotspot is a stationary plume of magma rising from the mantle. As a plate moves over it, the hotspot punches through to form a volcano; the plate carries the older volcano away and a new one forms over the hotspot, producing a chain of volcanic islands. The chain records the direction of plate motion, and the islands get older with distance from the active hotspot, exactly as plate tectonics predicts.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b><br>- 0.5 marks: a chain of volcanic islands forms.<br>- 0.5 marks: the chain shows the direction of plate motion.<br>- 0.5 marks: the hotspot is stationary while the plate moves over it.<br>- 0.5 marks: island age increases with distance from the hotspot.<br><br>",{});}]),["A fixed plume plus a moving plate leaves a dated trail."]);
// Source: June 2009 public exam, item 63(d).
E("es-u4-cr-q23",4,["EA12.4.23"],3,"var",varset([function(){return textq("A volcano is discovered in the middle of the Australian-Indian Plate, far from any plate boundary. Explain how this volcano most likely formed and describe its shape and lava.",["A shield volcano formed over a stationary hotspot: molten rock upwelling from the mantle melts through the plate, and its low-viscosity basaltic lava flows easily, building a broad volcano with gentle slopes."],"<b>Model answer:</b> Away from any boundary, the volcano most likely formed over a hotspot. A stationary plume of molten rock rising from the mantle melts and burns through the plate above it. The magma is basaltic, with low viscosity, so the lava flows long distances before solidifying. The result is a shield volcano: very broad, with gentle slopes built from many fluid lava flows.<br><br><b>Marking points (3 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: identifying a shield volcano formed at a hotspot.<br>- 1 mark: the stationary upwelling melting or burning through the plate.<br>- 1 mark: low-viscosity basaltic lava producing the broad, low shape.<br><br>",{});}]),["Mid-plate volcanism points to a hotspot, not a boundary."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.25: Volcanic interactions with the spheres ----- */
// Source: June 2006 public exam, item 64(b).
E("es-u4-cr-q24",4,["EA12.4.25"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Describe two ways in which volcanic eruptions affect Earth's atmosphere.",["Any two of: eruptions add gases such as SO2, CO2 and water vapour to the atmosphere; the original atmosphere formed partly by outgassing from volcanoes; ash and dust cool the climate by blocking sunlight; blocked sunlight interrupts photosynthesis."],"<b>Model answer:</b> Any two of the following: (1) Eruptions release gases such as sulphur dioxide, carbon dioxide and water vapour into the atmosphere; over geologic time, outgassing from volcanoes helped form the atmosphere itself. (2) Ash and dust ejected into the upper atmosphere block incoming sunlight, cooling the climate. (3) By blocking sunlight, large eruptions can also interrupt photosynthesis.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark for each of two valid effects.<br><br>",{});}]),["Gases go up; sunlight comes down less."]);
// Source: June 2009 public exam, item 63(e).
E("es-u4-cr-q25",4,["EA12.4.25"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Explain how volcanic activity can both cool Earth's climate in the short term and warm it in the long term.",["Cooling: material ejected into the atmosphere reflects incoming solar radiation. Warming: eruptions release greenhouse gases, carbon dioxide and water vapour, which trap outgoing radiation."],"<b>Model answer:</b> Cooling: ash, dust and sulphur gases ejected high into the atmosphere reflect incoming solar radiation back to space, lowering surface temperatures for months to years. Warming: eruptions also release greenhouse gases, carbon dioxide and water vapour, which absorb outgoing radiation and trap heat; sustained volcanism over geologic time adds these gases and warms the climate.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: the cooling mechanism (ejected material reflects solar radiation).<br>- 1 mark: the warming mechanism (greenhouse gases trap radiation).<br><br>",{});}]),["One effect reflects sunlight; the other traps heat."]);
// Source: August 2008 public exam, item 64(c).
E("es-u4-cr-q26",4,["EA12.4.25"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Explain two ways in which prolonged volcanic activity could harm the biosphere.",["Any two of: ash and gases pollute water supplies; crops and plants are destroyed, disrupting food webs; ash in the atmosphere blocks sunlight for years, reducing photosynthesis."],"<b>Model answer:</b> Any two of the following: (1) Ash and volcanic gases pollute water supplies, harming aquatic and land organisms that depend on them. (2) Crops and vegetation are buried or destroyed, and the food webs that depend on them are affected. (3) Ash suspended in the atmosphere can block sunlight for years, cutting photosynthesis and the food supply built on it.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark for each of two valid harms.<br><br>",{});}]),["Follow the ash: into water, onto crops, across the sky."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.4: Evidence for plate tectonics ----- */
// Source: August 2008 public exam, item 63(k).
E("es-u4-cr-q27",4,["EA12.4.4"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Explain how paleomagnetism provides evidence for the theory of plate tectonics.",["Apparent polar wandering paths for different continents coincide when the continents are reassembled, and magnetic reversals are recorded as symmetric stripes in the ocean crust on both sides of mid-ocean ridges, a tape recording of seafloor spreading (Vine and Matthews)."],"<b>Model answer:</b> Paleomagnetism gives two lines of evidence. First, the apparent polar wandering paths recorded in rocks of different continents do not agree while the continents are apart, but the paths coincide when the continents are reassembled, showing the continents moved. Second, Earth's magnetic field has reversed many times, and the reversals are recorded in the ocean crust as stripes of normal and reversed polarity, symmetric about mid-ocean ridges: the ocean floor is a tape recording of seafloor spreading, as Vine and Matthews proposed.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: polar wandering paths coinciding when continents are rejoined.<br>- 1 mark: magnetic reversal stripes symmetric about ridges recording seafloor spreading.<br><br>",{});}]),["Rocks remember the field; the stripes remember the spreading."]);

/* ----- EA12.9.1: Evidence for continental drift ----- */
E("es-u4-91-q01",4,["EA12.9.1"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Which evidence was used by Alfred Wegener to support the Continental Drift theory?","jigsaw fit of the continents",["movement along faults","sea stacks along shorelines","shallow-focus earthquakes at ridges"],"Wegener's case rested on how well the continents fit together, along with matching fossils, rock types and ancient climates across oceans. Fault movement, sea stacks and ridge earthquakes belong to the later plate tectonic evidence.",{});}]),["Wegener argued from how the continents match."]);
E("es-u4-91-q02",4,["EA12.9.1"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Identical fossils of the freshwater reptile Mesosaurus are found only in Brazil and in South Africa. Why did Wegener treat this as evidence for continental drift?","The two continents were once joined, so the reptile lived on a single landmass.",["The reptile swam across the Atlantic Ocean from one continent to the other.","Ocean currents carried the reptile's remains from one continent to the other.","The fossils formed independently in the same way on both continents by chance."],"Mesosaurus was a freshwater animal that could not have crossed an ocean. Finding its fossils on two continents separated by the Atlantic makes sense only if South America and Africa were once joined in one landmass.",{});}]),["Could a freshwater reptile cross an ocean?"]);

/* ----- EA12.9.2: Development of the plate tectonic theory ----- */
E("es-u4-92-q01",4,["EA12.9.2"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Who proposed the theory of continental drift?","Alfred Wegener",["Arthur Holmes","James Hutton","Tuzo Wilson"],"Alfred Wegener proposed continental drift in 1915, arguing that the continents had once formed a single supercontinent. Holmes later proposed a mechanism, Hutton worked a century earlier on geologic time, and Wilson contributed to plate tectonics in the 1960s.",{});}]),["The drift theory has one name attached to it."]);
E("es-u4-92-q02",4,["EA12.9.2"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Which occurred before Pangaea?","Rodinia",["Eurasia","Gondwanaland","Laurasia"],"Rodinia was an earlier supercontinent that broke apart long before Pangaea assembled. Gondwanaland and Laurasia were the two halves Pangaea split into, and Eurasia is a modern landmass.",{});}]),["Supercontinents formed more than once."]);
E("es-u4-92-q03",4,["EA12.9.2"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Which is the most recent supercontinent to have existed on Earth?","Pangaea",["the Appalachian Mountains","the Iapetus Ocean","Rodinia"],"Pangaea, the supercontinent Wegener proposed, existed about 200 million years ago and is the most recent one. Rodinia is far older, the Iapetus Ocean closed as Pangaea formed, and the Appalachians are a mountain range built during its assembly.",{});}]),["Wegener's supercontinent is the recent one."]);

/* ----- EA12.9.3: Wegener and Holmes ----- */
E("es-u4-93-q01",4,["EA12.9.3"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Why was the Theory of Continental Drift initially rejected by most scientists?","It did not propose a mechanism for how the continents moved.",["It claimed the continents were fixed permanently in their present positions.","It provided too much fossil evidence for scientists of the time to evaluate.","It relied entirely on computer models that had not yet been tested at sea."],"Wegener's evidence was strong, but he could not explain what force could move continents; his suggestion of tidal forces from the Moon was far too weak. Without a mechanism, the theory was rejected until Holmes proposed mantle convection and seafloor spreading was discovered.",{});}]),["The evidence convinced few without an engine."]);
E("es-u4-93-q02",4,["EA12.9.3"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Which scientist proposed mantle convection to support the theory of continental drift?","Arthur Holmes",["Alfred Wegener","James Hutton","Tuzo Wilson"],"Arthur Holmes proposed in the 1950s that convection currents in the mantle could carry the continents, supplying the mechanism Wegener's theory lacked.",{});}]),["One scientist supplied the missing mechanism."]);
E("es-u4-93-q03",4,["EA12.9.3"],2,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Which best represents the statement below? \"Plate tectonics, instead of continental drift, is accepted by scientists to explain the origins of mountains, earthquakes and volcanic activity through the movement of crustal plates.\"","paradigm",["evidence","hypothesis","law"],"A paradigm is the accepted framework through which scientists explain a whole field of observations. Plate tectonics replaced continental drift as that framework for geology.",{});}]),["A whole framework replaced an older one."]);
E("es-u4-93-q04",4,["EA12.9.3"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Which sequence represents the logical progression of scientific thought in Earth science?","Continental Drift → Plate Tectonics",["Catastrophism → Uniformitarianism","Plate Tectonics → Continental Drift","Uniformitarianism → Continental Drift"],"Continental drift came first (1915) and was later replaced by the more complete theory of plate tectonics once a mechanism and seafloor evidence were found. The other sequences reverse the order or mix in unrelated ideas.",{});}]),["Which theory came first, and which replaced it?"]);

/* ----- EA12.9.4: Canadian contributions to science and technology ----- */
E("es-u4-94-q01",4,["EA12.9.4"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Canadian geophysicist J. Tuzo Wilson made a major contribution to plate tectonic theory in 1965 by proposing the existence of which feature?","transform faults",["hot spots in the mantle","magnetic reversal stripes","subduction zones"],"In 1965 Wilson proposed transform faults: a new class of fault that offsets mid-ocean ridges and connects segments of plate boundaries. The proposal, from a Canadian scientist, was a key test that plate tectonics passed.",{});}]),["Wilson's 1965 idea concerned a new kind of fault."]);
E("es-u4-94-q02",4,["EA12.9.4"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("J. Tuzo Wilson explained the Hawaiian island chain as the record of a plate moving over a stationary hot spot. Why is this considered a Canadian contribution to science?","Wilson, a Canadian, supplied the explanation that linked island chains to plate motion.",["The Hawaiian Islands lie within Canadian territorial waters in the Pacific Ocean.","The research vessels that surveyed the islands were built and crewed in Canada.","The hot spot itself was first detected by a satellite launched from Canada."],"The contribution is the idea, not the location: Canadian geophysicist J. Tuzo Wilson proposed that a fixed hot spot under a moving plate produces a chain of volcanoes that age in the direction of motion.",{});}]),["The contribution is the scientist and his idea."]);

/* ----- EA12.9.5: Canadian contributions to plate tectonics ----- */
E("es-u4-95-q01",4,["EA12.9.5"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Which scientist contributed to plate tectonic theory by suggesting that hot spots in the mantle create volcanic islands within plates?","Tuzo Wilson",["Alfred Wegener","Harry Hess","John McKenzie"],"J. Tuzo Wilson proposed that stationary hot spots in the mantle punch through moving plates to build volcanic island chains inside plates, away from boundaries.",{});}]),["The hot spot idea has one name attached."]);
E("es-u4-95-q02",4,["EA12.9.5"],2,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Along a hot spot island chain, the islands become older in one direction and only the youngest island is volcanically active. What did Wilson conclude from this pattern?","The plate is moving over a hot spot that stays fixed in the mantle.",["The hot spot is migrating through the mantle toward the youngest island.","The whole chain was formed at the same time by a single large eruption.","The islands are sinking because the plate beneath them is being subducted."],"A fixed hot spot burns a track into a moving plate: each volcano is carried away from the plume, goes extinct, and ages with distance, while a new volcano grows over the plume. The age progression is the record of the plate's motion.",{});}]),["Ages increase steadily along the chain; what is moving?"]);

/* ----- EA12.9.6: Science and technology in daily life ----- */
E("es-u4-96-q01",4,["EA12.9.6"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("GPS stations anchored to bedrock on opposite sides of the Atlantic Ocean measure the distance between North America and Europe increasing by about 2.5 cm each year. What does this measurement demonstrate directly?","The plates carrying the continents are moving apart today.",["The Atlantic Ocean is becoming shallower as sediment fills it in.","Sea level is falling equally on both sides of the ocean basin.","The continents are shrinking as their edges erode into the sea."],"GPS measures present-day plate motion directly: the Atlantic is widening because the North American and Eurasian plates are moving apart at the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, at roughly the rate fingernails grow.",{});}]),["The same satellite system that gives directions also tracks plates."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.1: Divergent plate boundaries ----- */
E("es-u4-41-q01",4,["EA12.4.1"],2,"var",varset([function(){var q=mcq("Which type of plate boundary is present at points A and B in the diagram below?","A: transform, B: divergent",["A: divergent, B: divergent","A: divergent, B: transform","A: transform, B: transform"],"At point A the ridge segments are offset and the plates slide past each other, a transform boundary. At point B new crust is forming as the plates separate, a divergent boundary.",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-41-jun06-35.png" alt="Block diagram of a mid-ocean ridge offset by a fault, with points A and B marked" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["One point is on a ridge; the other offsets it."]);
E("es-u4-41-q02",4,["EA12.4.1"],1,"var",varset([function(){var q=mcq("What type of plate boundary is shown in the diagram below?","divergent boundary",["convergent boundary","subduction zone","transform boundary"],"The cross-section shows two plates moving apart with magma rising between them, the signature of a divergent boundary where new crust forms.",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-41-jun07-37.png" alt="Cross-section of two plates moving apart with magma rising between them" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["Plates separating with magma between them."]);
E("es-u4-41-q03",4,["EA12.4.1"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Which occurs at a transform plate boundary?","sliding of plates along each other",["collision of plates","formation of crust","subduction"],"At a transform boundary, plates slide horizontally past each other. Crust is neither created, as at divergent boundaries, nor destroyed by subduction, as at convergent boundaries.",{});}]),["Transform means sideways."]);
E("es-u4-41-q04",4,["EA12.4.1"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("The San Andreas Fault in California is an example of which type of plate boundary?","transform",["convergent","divergent","subduction"],"Along the San Andreas Fault, the Pacific plate slides past the North American plate horizontally, making it a transform boundary.",{});}]),["Two plates grind sideways past each other in California."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.2: Convergent plate boundaries ----- */
E("es-u4-42-q01",4,["EA12.4.2"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("At which plate boundary are island arcs found?","oceanic - oceanic",["oceanic - continental","divergent","transform"],"Island arcs form where one oceanic plate subducts beneath another oceanic plate; melting above the subducting slab builds a curved chain of volcanic islands.",{});}]),["An arc of volcanic islands needs one ocean plate diving under another."]);
E("es-u4-42-q02",4,["EA12.4.2"],2,"var",varset([function(){var q=mcq("Which best describes plates A and B below?","A: continental, B: oceanic",["A: continental, B: continental","A: oceanic, B: continental","A: oceanic, B: oceanic"],"Plate A carries thick, light continental crust, while plate B is the thinner, denser oceanic plate that is descending beneath it.",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-42-jun06-34.png" alt="Cross-section of two plates labelled A and B, with B descending beneath A" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["Which plate is sinking? That one is oceanic."]);
E("es-u4-42-q03",4,["EA12.4.2"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Which mountain range was formed by the collision of two continental plates?","Himalayan Mountains",["Aleutian Islands","Hawaiian Islands","Rocky Mountains"],"The Himalayas are still rising where the Indian plate collides with the Eurasian plate, continent against continent. The Aleutians are an island arc, Hawaii is a hot spot chain, and the Rockies formed at an ocean-continent margin.",{});}]),["Continent meets continent: neither can subduct easily."]);
E("es-u4-42-q04",4,["EA12.4.2"],2,"var",varset([function(){var q=mcq("Which type of plate collision is shown in the diagram below?","continent - continent",["continent - ocean","ocean - ocean","transform"],"The block diagram shows the Indian plate driving into Asia, crumpling both continental margins upward into the Himalayas: a continent-continent collision.",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-42-jun07-36.png" alt="Block diagram of the Indian plate colliding with Asia to form the Himalayas" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["Look at what kind of crust is on both sides."]);
E("es-u4-42-q05",4,["EA12.4.2"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Why does oceanic crust sink below continental crust at a convergent boundary?","Oceanic crust is more dense than continental crust.",["Oceanic crust is much older than continental crust.","Oceanic crust is thicker than continental crust.","Oceanic crust is warmer than continental crust."],"Oceanic crust is made of denser rock (basalt and gabbro) than continental crust (granite), so at a collision the oceanic plate sinks into the mantle in subduction. Oceanic crust is actually younger and thinner.",{});}]),["Subduction is decided by density."]);
E("es-u4-42-q06",4,["EA12.4.2"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("At which location is oceanic crust consumed?","convergent boundary",["divergent boundary","hotspot","ridge"],"Oceanic crust is consumed at convergent boundaries, where it subducts into the mantle and is recycled. At ridges and other divergent boundaries new crust is created instead.",{});}]),["Consumed means destroyed: where does crust dive down?"]);
E("es-u4-42-q07",4,["EA12.4.2"],2,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("A region is found to contain folding and areas with abundant outcrops of silica-rich rocks. Which best describes what happened to create the geology of this region?","ocean closed",["hot spot activity","ocean opened","transform movement"],"Folding records compression, and silica-rich rocks are continental-type rocks. Together they describe two continents colliding as the ocean between them closed, crumpling the margin into folded mountains.",{});}]),["Folding means squeezing; silica-rich means continental."]);
E("es-u4-42-q08",4,["EA12.4.2"],1,"var",varset([function(){var q=mcq("Which type of plate boundary and force is illustrated in the diagram below?","convergent - compressional",["convergent - tensional","divergent - compressional","divergent - tensional"],"The plates in the diagram are moving toward each other, so the boundary is convergent and the force squeezing the crust is compressional.",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-41-aug08-43.png" alt="Block diagram of two plates moving toward each other" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["Arrows pointing together mean one force type."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.3: Rift valleys ----- */
E("es-u4-43-q01",4,["EA12.4.3"],1,"var",varset([function(){var q=mcq("Which geologic feature is formed in the cross-section below?","rift valley",["mid-ocean ridge","transform fault","trench"],"The cross-section shows a block of crust dropped down between normal faults where the crust is being pulled apart, forming a rift valley.",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-41-jun06-33.png" alt="Cross-section of a dropped crustal block between faults, with a key" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["A dropped block between faults, on land."]);
E("es-u4-43-q02",4,["EA12.4.3"],2,"var",varset([function(){var q=mcq("The four diagrams below show stages in the development of a divergent boundary on a continent. Which sequence shows the correct order of development?","4, 2, 3, 1",["1, 2, 3, 4","2, 4, 1, 3","4, 3, 2, 1"],"Development begins with the crust doming and cracking (4), then a rift valley forms as blocks drop (2), the sea floods the rift (3), and finally a new ocean basin with a mid-ocean ridge opens (1).",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-43-final-52.png" alt="Four panels showing stages of a continent splitting apart" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["Start with cracking crust; end with a new ocean."]);
E("es-u4-43-q03",4,["EA12.4.3"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("The East African Rift is a long valley where the African plate is slowly splitting into two plates. Which processes are forming the rift valley?","Tensional forces stretch and thin the crust, which faults and drops down between normal faults.",["Compressional forces squeeze the crust, which folds upward into a high mountain valley.","Shear forces slide the crust sideways, which grinds a straight valley along a fault.","Cooling of the crust makes it contract and wrinkle, which sags into a broad valley."],"A rift valley is the surface expression of divergence on land: tension stretches and thins the crust until blocks drop down along normal faults, as in East Africa today.",{});}]),["East Africa is divergence happening on a continent."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.4: Evidence for plate tectonics ----- */
E("es-u4-44-q01",4,["EA12.4.4"],2,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Which is evidence for plates sinking back into Earth's interior as rigid slabs?","deep focus earthquakes",["hot-spot volcanoes","rifting at mid-ocean ridges","transform faults"],"Deep focus earthquakes occur hundreds of kilometres down along inclined zones (Wadati-Benioff zones) that trace a cold, rigid slab of plate descending into the mantle. The other features occur at or near the surface.",{});}]),["Which evidence comes from far below the surface?"]);
E("es-u4-44-q02",4,["EA12.4.4"],2,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Which best explains why region 1 of the mid-Atlantic ridge has wider magnetic stripes than those found nearby in region 2?","In region 1, sea floor spreading was at a greater rate.",["In region 1, sea floor spreading was at a slower rate.","There is less rhyolite in region 1.","There is less rhyolite in region 2."],"Magnetic stripes record time intervals between reversals. If spreading is faster, more crust forms during one polarity interval, so the stripe is wider. Stripe width therefore measures spreading rate.",{});}]),["A stripe's width is rate multiplied by time."]);
E("es-u4-44-q03",4,["EA12.4.4"],2,"var",varset([function(){var q=mcq("The diagram below represents a sea floor at a mid-ocean ridge. What does the pattern of magnetic stripes on either side of the ridge show?","The ocean floor records Earth's magnetic reversals as it spreads away from the ridge.",["The ocean floor is the same age everywhere because it formed in a single event.","The stripes are layers of sediment deposited in alternating coarse and fine bands.","The pattern shows that iron-rich lava flows alternate with iron-poor lava flows."],"As new crust solidifies at the ridge it locks in the current magnetic polarity. Reversals since then are recorded as matching stripes on both sides, the tape recording of seafloor spreading.",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-44-aug06-41.png" alt="Sea floor at a mid-ocean ridge with a striped magnetic pattern" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["The stripes match on both sides of the ridge."]);
E("es-u4-44-q04",4,["EA12.4.4"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Which supports the concept of sea floor spreading based on evidence collected in the 1960's?","age of oceanic crust increases away from mid-oceanic ridge",["glaciation in South America and Africa","jigsaw fit of the continents","similar fossils found on both sides of the Atlantic Ocean"],"Deep-ocean drilling in the 1960s dated the ocean floor and found the crust gets older with distance from the ridge, exactly as seafloor spreading predicts. The other choices are Wegener's older continental drift evidence.",{});}]),["Drilling dated the floor; what pattern did the ages show?"]);
E("es-u4-44-q05",4,["EA12.4.4"],2,"var",varset([function(){var q=mcq("What do the labelled stripes in the diagram below represent?","magnetic reversals recorded in the oceanic crust",["bands of sediment of different ages deposited on the sea floor","layers of basalt and gabbro alternating in the oceanic crust","paths of wandering taken by the magnetic pole over time"],"The stripes are bands of oceanic crust magnetized in normal and reversed polarity. Their symmetry about the ridge records both the reversal history and the spreading of the sea floor.",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-44-aug08-44.png" alt="Oceanic crust with stripes labelled normal and reverse polarity" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["The labels name the two polarities."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.5: Geology of Newfoundland and Labrador ----- */
E("es-u4-45-q01",4,["EA12.4.5"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("What type of plate boundary is associated with volcanic island arcs in central Newfoundland?","convergent",["divergent","normal","transform"],"Central Newfoundland's island arc rocks formed above a subduction zone as the Iapetus Ocean closed, so the boundary was convergent.",{});}]),["Island arcs grow above subduction zones."]);
E("es-u4-45-q02",4,["EA12.4.5"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Which is the best area on the island of Newfoundland to collect rocks formed by island arcs?","central",["eastern","northern","western"],"The island arc volcanoes of the ancient Iapetus Ocean were caught in the middle when the ocean closed, so their rocks are found in central Newfoundland, between the western (North American) and eastern (African) zones.",{});}]),["The arcs were in the ocean that closed: the middle zone."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.6: Deformation, force and stress ----- */
E("es-u4-46-q01",4,["EA12.4.6"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("In geology, what is stress?","the force applied per unit area of a rock",["the total force exerted on a rock regardless of its size","the permanent change in a rock's shape after it is squeezed","the distance a fault block moves during an earthquake"],"Stress is force divided by the area it acts on. The same force produces greater stress on a smaller area, which is why stress, not force alone, decides how a rock deforms.",{});}]),["Stress includes the area the force acts on."]);
E("es-u4-46-q02",4,["EA12.4.6"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("What does deformation mean in geology?","a change in the shape or volume of rock in response to stress",["the breaking of rock into fragments during an earthquake","the cooling and contraction of magma beneath the surface","the wearing away of rock at Earth's surface by erosion"],"Deformation is any change in a rock's original shape or volume caused by stress. It includes elastic stretching, permanent folding (ductile deformation) and fracturing (brittle deformation). Most deformation happens at plate margins, where stresses are greatest.",{});}]),["Deformation is about shape change under stress."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.7: Types of stress ----- */
E("es-u4-47-q01",4,["EA12.4.7"],2,"var",varset([function(){var q=mcq("Which best indicates how two plates moved to form the structures below?","Direction: away from each other; Force: tensional",["Direction: away from each other; Force: compressional","Direction: towards each other; Force: compressional","Direction: towards each other; Force: tensional"],"The structures are fault blocks that dropped as the crust was pulled apart: the plates moved away from each other under tensional forces.",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-47-jun06-37.png" alt="Profile of fault blocks dropped between faults" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["Dropped blocks mean the crust was pulled apart."]);
E("es-u4-47-q02",4,["EA12.4.7"],2,"var",varset([function(){var q=mcq("Which type of force produced the feature in the diagram below?","tension",["compression","fracture","shear"],"The feature formed as the crust was pulled apart, which is tension. Compression pushes together, and shear slides sideways.",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-47-jun07-42.png" alt="Block diagram with arrows pulling the crust in opposite directions" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["The arrows pull in opposite directions."]);
E("es-u4-47-q03",4,["EA12.4.7"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Which type of fault results from shear stress?","strike-slip",["normal","reverse","thrust"],"Shear stress acts parallel to a surface, sliding the two sides past each other horizontally, which produces a strike-slip fault. Compression produces reverse and thrust faults; tension produces normal faults.",{});}]),["Shear means sliding sideways."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.8: Types of deformation ----- */
E("es-u4-48-q01",4,["EA12.4.8"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("A rock is squeezed by a small stress and springs back to its original shape when the stress is removed. Which type of deformation is this?","elastic deformation",["brittle deformation","ductile deformation","permanent deformation"],"Elastic deformation is recoverable: like a stretched elastic band, the rock returns to its original shape when the stress is removed, as long as its elastic limit is not passed.",{});}]),["The key word is springs back."]);
E("es-u4-48-q02",4,["EA12.4.8"],2,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Deep in the crust, hot rock under high confining pressure is slowly compressed and bends into folds without breaking. Which type of deformation is this?","ductile deformation",["brittle deformation","elastic deformation","faulting"],"Ductile deformation is permanent bending or flowing in the solid state, without fracture. It happens deep in the crust, where high temperature and confining pressure let rock flow slowly instead of breaking.",{});}]),["Hot, deep and slow means flow, not fracture."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.9: Factors affecting deformation ----- */
E("es-u4-49-q01",4,["EA12.4.9"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Which rock type is most easily deformed?","sedimentary",["basalt","gabbro","granite"],"Sedimentary rocks, especially layered types such as shale, deform more easily than hard, crystalline igneous rocks such as granite, gabbro and basalt.",{});}]),["Layered rocks bend more readily than crystalline ones."]);
E("es-u4-49-q02",4,["EA12.4.9"],2,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("A cold rock near Earth's surface is struck by a sudden, rapid stress. How is it most likely to deform?","It will fracture in brittle deformation.",["It will bend into folds in ductile deformation.","It will stretch and then recover its original shape.","It will flow slowly in the solid state without breaking."],"Cold rock near the surface, stressed quickly, passes its elastic limit and breaks: brittle deformation. Warm temperatures, high confining pressure and slow stress favour ductile flow instead; the rate and conditions decide which occurs.",{});}]),["Cold, shallow and fast points one way."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.10: Faulting ----- */
E("es-u4-410-q01",4,["EA12.4.10"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("What is faulting?","the breaking of rock along a fracture, with movement of the rock on either side",["the bending of rock layers into arches and troughs without breaking","the slow flowing of rock in the solid state under heat and pressure","the elastic stretching of rock that recovers when stress is removed"],"Faulting occurs when stress exceeds the rock's strength and it fractures, and the blocks on either side of the fracture (the fault) move relative to each other. Bending without breaking is folding.",{});}]),["A fault needs a break and movement."]);
E("es-u4-410-q02",4,["EA12.4.10"],3,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Continual compression is applied to rock in two places: rapidly, at shallow depth, and slowly, deep in the crust. What is the most likely result in each place?","Faulting at shallow depth; folding deep in the crust.",["Folding at shallow depth; faulting deep in the crust.","Faulting in both places, because compression always breaks rock.","Folding in both places, because compression always bends rock."],"Shallow rock is cold and brittle, so rapid compression breaks it: faulting. Deep rock is hot and under confining pressure, so slow compression bends it: folding. Depth, temperature and rate decide which response compression produces.",{});}]),["Same force, different depth and rate."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.11: Faulting ----- */
E("es-u4-411-q01",4,["EA12.4.11"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("What is a low angle (less than 45 degrees) compressional fault called?","thrust",["graben","horst","normal"],"A thrust fault is a low-angle reverse fault: compression pushes the hanging wall up and over the footwall along a gently dipping plane. A graben is a dropped fault block and a horst a raised one; both are tensional features, and a normal fault dips steeply.",{});}]),["Low angle plus compression is the definition."]);
E("es-u4-411-q02",4,["EA12.4.11"],2,"var",varset([function(){var q=mcq("What type of fault is shown in the diagram below?","reverse fault",["normal fault","strike-slip fault","transform fault"],"The hanging wall has moved up relative to the footwall along the fault plane, which is a reverse fault, produced by compression.",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-411-jun08-36.png" alt="Cross-section of a fault with the hanging wall raised" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["Hanging wall up means one fault type."]);
E("es-u4-411-q03",4,["EA12.4.11"],2,"var",varset([function(){var q=mcq("Which pairing correctly identifies the stress at point X and the fault at point Y in the diagram below?","X: shear (transform), Y: tensional (normal)",["X: compressional (reverse), Y: shear (transform)","X: tensional (normal), Y: compressional (reverse)","X: shear (transform), Y: compressional (reverse)"],"At X the blocks slide sideways, shear stress at a transform fault. At Y the hanging wall has dropped as the crust pulled apart, tensional stress at a normal fault.",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-411-jun08-38.png" alt="Block diagram with points X and Y on different faults" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["One point slides sideways; the other dropped down."]);
E("es-u4-411-q04",4,["EA12.4.11"],2,"var",varset([function(){var q=mcq("Which force and fault is shown in the diagram below?","Force: tension; Fault: normal",["Force: compression; Fault: normal","Force: compression; Fault: reverse","Force: tension; Fault: reverse"],"The hanging wall has dropped down as the crust was pulled apart: tension producing a normal fault.",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-411-jun09-39.png" alt="Cross-section of a fault with the hanging wall dropped down" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["Hanging wall down pairs with one force."]);
E("es-u4-411-q05",4,["EA12.4.11"],2,"var",varset([function(){var q=mcq("Which fault is shown in the cross-section below?","reverse",["normal","thrust","transform"],"The hanging wall has been pushed up relative to the footwall along a steeply dipping fault plane, which is a reverse fault. A thrust fault is also compressional but has a low-angle plane.",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-411-aug07-39.png" alt="Cross-section of a fault with the hanging wall pushed up" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["Check the angle as well as the motion."]);
E("es-u4-411-q06",4,["EA12.4.11"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Which type of fault is formed by tension?","normal",["reversal","thrust","transform"],"Tension pulls the crust apart and the hanging wall drops, forming a normal fault. Thrust faults come from compression, and transform faults from shear.",{});}]),["Tension pulls apart; which fault drops a block?"]);
E("es-u4-411-q07",4,["EA12.4.11"],2,"var",varset([function(){var q=mcq("Which type of fault is illustrated below?","strike-slip",["dip-slip","reverse","thrust"],"The two blocks have moved horizontally past each other with no vertical offset, which is a strike-slip fault. Dip-slip faults (normal and reverse) move blocks up or down along the dip of the plane.",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-411-aug09-41.png" alt="Two blocks offset horizontally along a fault" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["All the motion here is sideways."]);
E("es-u4-411-q08",4,["EA12.4.11"],2,"var",varset([function(){var q=mcq("The diagram below shows a fault at a convergent plate boundary. Which pairing of stress and faulting is correct?","Stress: compression; Faulting: reverse",["Stress: shear; Faulting: normal","Stress: tension; Faulting: normal","Stress: tension; Faulting: reverse"],"At a convergent boundary the plates push together, so the stress is compression, and compression drives the hanging wall up: reverse faulting, as the diagram shows.",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-411-final-57.png" alt="Block diagram of a reverse fault with compression arrows" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["Convergent boundaries squeeze; the wall went up."]);
E("es-u4-411-q09",4,["EA12.4.11"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("In a strike-slip fault, how do the blocks move relative to each other?","They slide horizontally past each other along the fault.",["The hanging wall moves down along the dip of the fault plane.","The hanging wall moves up along the dip of the fault plane.","They rotate away from each other around a vertical axis."],"Strike-slip faults are dominated by horizontal motion: shear stress slides the blocks sideways, as along the San Andreas Fault. Up-or-down motion along the dip defines dip-slip faults instead.",{});}]),["Strike-slip is named for movement along the strike."]);
E("es-u4-411-q10",4,["EA12.4.11"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("What kind of movement defines a dip-slip fault?","vertical movement of blocks along the dip of the fault plane",["horizontal movement of blocks along the strike of the fault plane","rotational movement of blocks around the fault's midpoint","no movement at all until an earthquake releases the stress"],"Dip-slip faults move blocks up or down the slope (dip) of the fault plane: normal faults (hanging wall down) and reverse faults (hanging wall up) are both dip-slip. Horizontal movement along the strike is strike-slip.",{});}]),["Dip is the slope of the plane; the motion follows it."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.12: Folding ----- */
E("es-u4-412-q01",4,["EA12.4.12"],2,"var",varset([function(){var q=mcq("Which type of fold is indicated by the arrow in the diagram below?","syncline",["anticline","overturned","recumbent"],"The arrow points into the trough of the fold, where the layers bend downward: a syncline. An anticline arches upward, in the shape of the letter A.",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-412-jun06-38.png" alt="Train of folds with an arrow pointing into a trough" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["The arrow points into a trough, not an arch."]);
E("es-u4-412-q02",4,["EA12.4.12"],2,"var",varset([function(){var q=mcq("Which feature would be found in the same tectonic environment as shown in the diagram below?","folded rocks",["drumlin","rift valley","shield volcano"],"The diagram shows a compressional (convergent) environment, where the crust is squeezed. Folded rocks form in the same environment; rift valleys form under tension and drumlins are glacial deposits.",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-43-aug06-40.png" alt="Block diagram of a faulted and folded terrain" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["Match the environment, not just the picture."]);
E("es-u4-412-q03",4,["EA12.4.12"],2,"var",varset([function(){var q=mcq("What produced the structure in the diagram below?","compressional forces",["faulting","shear forces","tensional forces"],"The structure is an anticline, an upward arch of folded rock. Folds form when compressional forces slowly squeeze rock layers until they bend.",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-412-aug08-39.png" alt="Block diagram of an upfolded rock structure" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["An arch of rock layers means squeezing."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.13: Earthquakes ----- */
E("es-u4-413-q01",4,["EA12.4.13"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Which is the point of origin of an earthquake?","focus",["epicentre","epicycle","fault"],"The focus (hypocentre) is the point inside Earth where the rock first breaks and the earthquake originates. The epicentre is the point on the surface directly above it.",{});}]),["Origin means underground."]);
E("es-u4-413-q02",4,["EA12.4.13"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("What is the point on Earth's surface directly above an earthquake's origin?","epicenter",["aftershock","focus","foreshock"],"The epicentre is the surface point directly above the focus, where the earthquake starts underground. Foreshocks and aftershocks are smaller earthquakes before and after the main shock.",{});}]),["Directly above the focus, on the surface."]);
E("es-u4-413-q03",4,["EA12.4.13"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("What most often triggers a tsunami?","earthquake",["avalanche","hurricane","land slide"],"Most tsunamis are triggered by undersea earthquakes that suddenly lift or drop the sea floor, displacing the water column above it. Landslides can also cause them, but earthquakes are the most common trigger.",{});}]),["A sudden shift of the sea floor starts the wave."]);
E("es-u4-413-q04",4,["EA12.4.13"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("After a major earthquake, a series of smaller earthquakes follows in the same area over the next several days. What are these smaller earthquakes called?","aftershocks",["epicentres","foreshocks","tremors"],"Aftershocks are smaller earthquakes that follow the main shock as the crust adjusts to its new position. Foreshocks, by contrast, come before the main earthquake.",{});}]),["Before is fore; after is after."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.14: Earthquake locations and depths ----- */
E("es-u4-414-q01",4,["EA12.4.14"],2,"var",varset([function(){var q=mcq("At which location in the diagram below would the greatest number of shallow focus earthquakes occur?","B",["A","C","D"],"Shallow focus earthquakes are most numerous where plates first begin to descend at a subduction zone, before the slab angles deeper. Location B sits at that shallow part of the Wadati-Benioff zone.",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-414-jun08-42.png" alt="Cross-section of a subduction zone with locations A, B, C and D" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["Shallow means near where the slab starts down."]);
E("es-u4-414-q02",4,["EA12.4.14"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Where do the majority of earthquakes occur?","plate boundaries",["centre of continents","continental shelves","deep ocean basins"],"Most earthquakes occur along plate boundaries, where plates grind, collide or separate and stress builds up along faults. Plate interiors, such as the centres of continents, are comparatively quiet.",{});}]),["Earthquakes trace the edges of plates."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.15: Seismic waves ----- */
E("es-u4-415-q01",4,["EA12.4.15"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Why are S-waves not recorded on the side of Earth opposite an earthquake?","They cannot travel through liquids.",["They are absorbed by the crust before reaching the mantle.","They travel too slowly to reach the far side of Earth.","They change into P-waves when they enter the mantle."],"S-waves are shear waves and need a rigid, solid material to travel through. The liquid outer core stops them completely, creating the S-wave shadow zone on the far side of Earth.",{});}]),["One wave type is stopped by liquid."]);
E("es-u4-415-q02",4,["EA12.4.15"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Which earthquake wave can travel through both solids and liquids?","primary",["rayleigh","secondary","shear"],"Primary (P) waves are compressional: they squeeze and expand the material they pass through, which works in both solids and liquids. Secondary (S) waves are shear waves and travel only through solids.",{});}]),["Compression works in any material; shear needs solid."]);
E("es-u4-415-q03",4,["EA12.4.15"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Which wave is the fastest and moves in a push-pull motion?","primary",["L","S","surface"],"Primary (P) waves are the fastest seismic waves and move by compression and expansion, a push-pull motion, which is why they are recorded first at a station.",{});}]),["Fastest and first to arrive are the same wave."]);
E("es-u4-415-q04",4,["EA12.4.15"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Which layer of Earth is liquid and blocks the movement of S-waves?","outer core",["crust","inner core","mantle"],"The outer core is liquid iron and nickel. Because S-waves cannot travel through liquid, their absence beyond the shadow zone is the main evidence that the outer core is molten; the inner core is solid.",{});}]),["The shadow zone points to one liquid layer."]);
E("es-u4-415-q05",4,["EA12.4.15"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("What happens to the speed of seismic waves when they pass from the crust to the mantle?","speeds up",["remains the same","slows down","stops"],"Mantle rock is denser and more rigid than crustal rock, so seismic waves speed up when they cross into the mantle. This sudden increase at the boundary (the Mohorovicic discontinuity) is how the boundary was discovered.",{});}]),["Denser, more rigid rock carries waves faster."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.16: Magnitude and intensity scales ----- */
E("es-u4-416-q01",4,["EA12.4.16"],2,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("An earthquake with a magnitude of 7.2 did not cause any buildings to collapse. How would this earthquake be rated on the Richter and Mercalli scales?","Richter: high; Mercalli: low",["Richter: high; Mercalli: high","Richter: low; Mercalli: high","Richter: low; Mercalli: low"],"Magnitude 7.2 is a high Richter rating, since the Richter scale measures the energy released at the source. The Mercalli scale measures intensity: observed effects and damage. With no buildings collapsed, the Mercalli rating is low.",{});}]),["One scale measures the quake; the other measures its effects."]);
E("es-u4-416-q02",4,["EA12.4.16"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Which scale measures earthquake intensity and is based on damage to structures?","Modified Mercalli",["Benioff","Moh's","Richter"],"The Modified Mercalli scale rates intensity from observations of damage and effects, using Roman numerals from I to XII. The Richter scale measures magnitude from instrument readings.",{});}]),["Damage-based and Roman numerals."]);
E("es-u4-416-q03",4,["EA12.4.16"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Which aspect of earthquakes is recorded by the Modified Mercalli Scale?","intensity",["amplitude","location","magnitude"],"The Modified Mercalli scale records intensity: the observed effects of an earthquake at a place, including damage. Magnitude, measured by the Richter scale, is a single value for the earthquake itself.",{});}]),["Mercalli is about effects at a location."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.17: Magnitude, amplitude and energy ----- */
E("es-u4-417-q01",4,["EA12.4.17"],2,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("How much greater is the amplitude of a magnitude 6 earthquake than a magnitude 4 earthquake?","100 times",["2 times","10 times","1000 times"],"Each whole step on the Richter scale multiplies wave amplitude by 10. Two steps (6 - 4 = 2) multiply it by 10 x 10 = 100.",{});}]),["Amplitude grows by 10 per step; count the steps."]);
E("es-u4-417-q02",4,["EA12.4.17"],2,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("An earthquake in Japan registered 7.5 on the Richter scale and another earthquake in Mexico measured 6.5. Which earthquake showed the greatest amplitude, and by how much?","Japan by 10 times",["Japan by 30 times","Mexico by 10 times","Mexico by 30 times"],"The larger magnitude has the greater amplitude. One step on the Richter scale (7.5 - 6.5) means 10 times the amplitude. (Energy would increase by about 30 times, but the question asks about amplitude.)",{});}]),["Amplitude and energy use different multipliers."]);
E("es-u4-417-q03",4,["EA12.4.17"],2,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("How does the energy released by an earthquake of magnitude 7 compare to the energy released by an earthquake of magnitude 4?","27,000 times more energy",["30 times more energy","90 times more energy","900 times more energy"],"Each magnitude step multiplies energy released by about 30. Three steps (7 - 4 = 3) multiply it by 30 x 30 x 30 = 27,000.",{});}]),["Energy grows by about 30 per step; count the steps."]);
E("es-u4-417-q04",4,["EA12.4.17"],2,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("How would the energy released by an earthquake of magnitude 6.5 be compared to the energy released by an earthquake of magnitude 3.5?","27,000 times more energy",["30 times more energy","90 times more energy","900 times more energy"],"The difference is 3 magnitude steps, and each step multiplies energy by about 30: 30 x 30 x 30 = 27,000 times more energy.",{});}]),["Same rule: about 30 per step, three steps."]);
E("es-u4-417-q05",4,["EA12.4.17"],3,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("An earthquake released 27,000 times more energy than an earthquake of magnitude 3.5. What was the magnitude of the larger earthquake?","6.5",["4.5","5.5","7.5"],"Energy multiplies by about 30 per magnitude step. 27,000 = 30 x 30 x 30, which is three steps above 3.5, giving 3.5 + 3 = 6.5.",{});}]),["Work backwards: how many factors of 30 make 27,000?"]);

/* ----- EA12.4.18: Locating an epicentre ----- */
E("es-u4-418-q01",4,["EA12.4.18"],2,"var",varset([function(){var q=mcq("If the difference in arrival times between P- and S-waves on the graph below is 2.5 minutes, what is the distance from the epicentre of the earthquake to the seismograph station?","1500 km",["600 km","100 km","2500 km"],"On the travel-time graph, find where the gap between the S curve and the P curve equals 2.5 minutes; that gap occurs at a distance of about 1500 km.",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-418-jun06-43.png" alt="Travel-time graph for P and S waves" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["Match the arrival gap to the gap between the curves."]);
E("es-u4-418-q02",4,["EA12.4.18"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("What is used to determine the distance from a seismograph station to the epicentre of an earthquake?","time between the arrival of P-waves and S-waves",["amplitude of the largest P-wave","amplitude of the largest S-wave","distance between the P-waves and S-waves"],"P-waves travel faster than S-waves, so the time gap between their arrivals grows with distance. A travel-time graph converts that gap into the distance to the epicentre.",{});}]),["The faster wave pulls ahead; the gap tells the distance."]);
E("es-u4-418-q03",4,["EA12.4.18"],2,"var",varset([function(){var q=mcq("What is the time difference between the arrival of the P-waves and the S-waves at Station A, as shown on the seismogram below?","5 minutes",["2 minutes","8 minutes","11 minutes"],"On the seismogram, the P-waves arrive first and the S-waves follow 5 minutes later. That arrival difference is what a travel-time graph converts into distance.",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-418-aug07-44.png" alt="Seismogram recorded at Station A" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["Read the two arrival times off the record and subtract."]);
E("es-u4-418-q04",4,["EA12.4.18"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("What is the minimum number of seismograph stations needed to locate the epicentre of an earthquake?","three",["one","two","four"],"Each station gives a distance, which defines a circle of possible locations. Two circles intersect at two points; a third station's circle pins the epicentre to the single point where all three meet.",{});}]),["Circles from stations must meet at one point."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.19: Volcanoes ----- */
E("es-u4-419-q01",4,["EA12.4.19"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Which type of volcano is the smallest with the steepest slope?","cinder",["composite","shield","strato"],"Cinder (ash and cinder) cones are built from loose pyroclastic fragments piled at the angle of repose, so they stay small but steep. Shield volcanoes are broad with gentle slopes, and composite (strato) volcanoes grow much larger.",{});}]),["Small and steep go together for one type."]);
E("es-u4-419-q02",4,["EA12.4.19"],2,"var",varset([function(){var q=mcq("Which is represented in the diagram below?","composite volcano",["cinder cone","pyroclastic cone","shield volcano"],"The diagram shows a cone built of alternating layers of ash and lava flows, the layered structure of a composite volcano.",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-419-aug06-44.png" alt="Volcano cross-section with alternating ash and lava layers labelled" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["Alternating ash and lava layers are the clue."]);
E("es-u4-419-q03",4,["EA12.4.19"],2,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("A volcano erupts runny, low-viscosity basaltic lava in quiet flows, while another erupts thick, high-viscosity magma in violent explosions. Which property of the magma is mainly responsible for the difference in eruption style?","its silica content, which controls viscosity",["its temperature alone, regardless of composition","the depth of the magma chamber below the volcano","the height of the volcano through which it erupts"],"Silica-rich magma is viscous: it traps gases until pressure builds to a violent release. Low-silica basaltic magma is fluid, lets gases escape, and erupts quietly. Composition, through viscosity, is the main control on eruption style.",{});}]),["Thick magma traps gas; runny magma lets it go."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.20: Eruptions and plate boundaries ----- */
E("es-u4-420-q01",4,["EA12.4.20"],2,"var",varset([function(){var q=mcq("Which type of volcano and plate boundary is present in the diagram below?","Volcano: composite; Plate Boundary: convergent",["Volcano: composite; Plate Boundary: divergent","Volcano: shield; Plate Boundary: convergent","Volcano: shield; Plate Boundary: divergent"],"The diagram shows a steep, layered cone erupting explosively above a subduction zone: a composite volcano at a convergent boundary.",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-420-jun06-47.png" alt="Erupting volcano above a subduction zone" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["Explosive and layered, above a sinking plate."]);
E("es-u4-420-q02",4,["EA12.4.20"],2,"var",varset([function(){var q=mcq("What type of plate boundary would produce the volcano below?","convergent",["divergent","hot spot","transform"],"The volcano in the diagram is a composite cone of alternating pyroclastic layers and lava flows, the type built above subduction zones at convergent boundaries.",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-420-jun08-46.png" alt="Block diagram of a composite volcano with pyroclastic layers and lava flows labelled" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["Layered cone, explosive history: which boundary?"]);
E("es-u4-420-q03",4,["EA12.4.20"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Which geological setting would be the site of violent volcanic eruptions?","convergent plate boundary",["divergent plate boundary","mid-oceanic hot spot","transform plate boundary"],"At convergent boundaries, subduction produces silica-rich, viscous magma that traps gas and erupts violently. Divergent boundaries and ocean hot spots erupt fluid basalt comparatively quietly.",{});}]),["Violence needs viscous, gas-rich magma."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.21: Rocks of volcanoes ----- */
E("es-u4-421-q01",4,["EA12.4.21"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("At which location is andesite most likely found?","convergent plate boundary",["divergent plate boundary","hot spot","transform plate boundary"],"Andesite, named for the Andes, is the characteristic volcanic rock of subduction zones at convergent boundaries, with an intermediate silica content between basalt and rhyolite.",{});}]),["Andesite is named for a subduction mountain range."]);
E("es-u4-421-q02",4,["EA12.4.21"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Which volcano will produce basaltic lava when it erupts?","shield",["cinder","composite","stratovolcano"],"Shield volcanoes are built almost entirely of fluid basaltic lava flows. Composite volcanoes (stratovolcanoes) erupt andesite, basalt and rhyolite, and cinder cones erupt basalt and scoria.",{});}]),["One type is basalt from base to summit."]);
E("es-u4-421-q03",4,["EA12.4.21"],2,"var",varset([function(){var q=mcq("Which landform at location X and rock type at location Y is shown in the diagram below?","X: island arc; Y: gabbro",["X: island arc; Y: basalt","X: volcanic arc; Y: basalt","X: volcanic arc; Y: gabbro"],"Location X is the chain of volcanic islands built above the subduction zone, an island arc. Location Y lies deep in the crust beneath it, where magma cools slowly into gabbro, the intrusive equivalent of basalt.",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-421-aug09-40.png" alt="Subduction cross-section with locations X and Y marked" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["Y is deep and slow-cooling: which rock forms there?"]);
E("es-u4-421-q04",4,["EA12.4.21"],2,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("What is the correct pairing of rock type and plate boundary?","Rock Type: basalt; Plate Boundary: ocean-ocean divergent",["Rock Type: gabbro; Plate Boundary: convergent","Rock Type: granite; Plate Boundary: divergent","Rock Type: gypsum; Plate Boundary: ocean-continent convergent"],"Basalt is the volcanic rock of divergent boundaries, including where two oceanic plates separate at a mid-ocean ridge. Granite forms in continental crust, and gypsum is a sedimentary evaporite, not a boundary rock.",{});}]),["Basalt belongs to the ridges."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.22: Lava types ----- */
E("es-u4-422-q01",4,["EA12.4.22"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("A basaltic lava flow cools with a smooth, ropy surface that looks like twisted rope. What is this type of lava called?","pahoehoe",["aa","pillow lava","scoria"],"Pahoehoe is fluid basaltic lava whose surface wrinkles into smooth, ropy folds as it flows. Aa, in contrast, is jagged and blocky.",{});}]),["Ropy and smooth names one type."]);
E("es-u4-422-q02",4,["EA12.4.22"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Which description matches aa lava?","a jagged, angular surface of rough, broken blocks",["a smooth, ropy surface that wrinkles as it flows","rounded, pillow-shaped masses formed under water","a frothy, gas-filled rock light enough to float"],"Aa forms when lava loses gas and stiffens as it moves, tearing its crust into jagged, angular blocks. The smooth ropy texture is pahoehoe, and the rounded underwater forms are pillow lava.",{});}]),["Aa is the rough one you would not walk on."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.23: Hotspots ----- */
E("es-u4-423-q01",4,["EA12.4.23"],2,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("A series of older, extinct volcanoes extends north from a large active volcano in the center of a tectonic plate. Which most likely formed all these volcanoes?","hot spot",["deep-ocean trench","mid-ocean ridge","plate boundary"],"The volcanoes sit in the middle of a plate, away from any boundary. A stationary hot spot under the moving plate built each volcano in turn, leaving a chain that ages away from the active end.",{});}]),["Mid-plate and arranged in an aging line."]);
E("es-u4-423-q02",4,["EA12.4.23"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Which is an example of hotspot volcanism?","Hawaiian Islands",["Aleutian Trench","Himalayan Mountains","San Andreas Fault"],"The Hawaiian Islands were built by a hot spot under the Pacific plate. The Aleutians are a subduction island arc, the Himalayas a continent-continent collision, and the San Andreas a transform fault.",{});}]),["One of these is mid-plate."]);
E("es-u4-423-q03",4,["EA12.4.23"],3,"var",varset([function(){var q=mcq("The cross-section below shows the direction of movement of an oceanic plate over a mantle hotspot. The age of volcano C is shown. What are the most likely ages of volcanoes B and D?","Volcano B: 5 million years old; Volcano D: 12 million years old",["Volcano B: 2 million years old; Volcano D: 6 million years old","Volcano B: 9 million years old; Volcano D: 9 million years old","Volcano B: 10 million years old; Volcano D: 4 million years old"],"Volcanoes age with distance from the hot spot, in the direction the plate has carried them. B lies a short distance past C, so it is slightly older; D lies much farther along the track, so it is the oldest.",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u4-423-jun07-39.png" alt="Cross-section of volcanoes A to D over a mantle hotspot" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["Age increases with distance from the plume."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.24: Lava plateaus ----- */
E("es-u4-424-q01",4,["EA12.4.24"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("How does a lava plateau form?","Huge volumes of fluid lava pour from long fissures and spread over the landscape in successive flows.",["A single central volcano erupts for millions of years, stacking lava into a high plateau.","Ash from explosive eruptions settles evenly over a region and hardens into flat layers.","A block of crust is uplifted between faults and then levelled flat by erosion."],"A lava plateau is built by fissure eruptions: cracks that reach deep toward the mantle release enormous volumes of runny basalt, which floods the surface flow after flow until a thick, flat plateau stands where valleys once were. It does not come from a crater or a single cone.",{});}]),["Fissures, not craters, flood whole landscapes."]);
E("es-u4-424-q02",4,["EA12.4.24"],2,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("A region is covered by a flat-topped stack of basalt flows, each about the same thickness, extending for hundreds of kilometres with no central volcano. What is the best interpretation?","Fissure eruptions from deep cracks flooded the area repeatedly, building a lava plateau.",["One shield volcano grew so wide that its slopes appear flat across the whole region.","Explosive eruptions from many small cinder cones merged into a single flat deposit.","The basalt was deposited as sediment in a shallow sea and later exposed by erosion."],"Uniform flows over a huge area, with no cone, are the signature of fissure-fed flood eruptions. A shield volcano, however broad, is thickest at its centre; these flows are not.",{});}]),["No cone, uniform thickness, vast area."]);

/* ----- EA12.9.7: Careers in Earth systems science ----- */
E("es-u4-97-q01",4,["EA12.9.7"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Who studies lava eruptions and rates of cooling?","volcanologist",["hydrologist","paleontologist","seismologist"],"A volcanologist studies volcanoes: eruptions, lava and how volcanic materials cool. A seismologist studies earthquakes, a hydrologist water, and a paleontologist ancient life.",{});}]),["The career name matches the volcano."]);
E("es-u4-97-q02",4,["EA12.9.7"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Which scientist studies earthquakes and the waves they generate to understand Earth's interior and assess seismic risk?","seismologist",["geomorphologist","petrologist","sedimentologist"],"A seismologist studies earthquakes and seismic waves, using them both to image Earth's interior and to assess where damaging earthquakes are likely. Petrologists study rocks, sedimentologists sediments, and geomorphologists landforms.",{});}]),["Seismic waves are this scientist's tool."]);
E("es-u4-97-q03",4,["EA12.9.7"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("A mining company needs to know how folded and faulted rock layers will behave before it opens a new mine. Which specialist should it hire?","structural geologist",["geochemist","sedimentologist","volcanologist"],"A structural geologist studies deformed rock: folds, faults and the stresses that produced them, exactly what mine planning in deformed terrain requires.",{});}]),["Folds and faults are one specialty's subject."]);


