/* ----- EA12.4.15: Describe properties of the different seismic waves ----- */
E("es-u4-415-q06",4,["EA12.4.15"],3,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Through Earth's crust, P-waves travel at about 6 km/s and S-waves at about 3.6 km/s. An earthquake occurs 360 km from a seismograph station. How much later than the P-waves do the S-waves arrive at the station?","40 seconds",["60 seconds","100 seconds","160 seconds"],"The P-waves cover the 360 km at 6 km/s, taking 360 / 6 = 60 seconds. The S-waves cover the same 360 km at 3.6 km/s, taking 360 / 3.6 = 100 seconds. The S-waves therefore arrive 100 - 60 = 40 seconds after the P-waves. The gap grows with distance, which is what makes it useful for locating an epicentre.",{});}]),["Find each wave's travel time separately, then compare the two times."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.18: Describe how seismographs and resulting seismograms are used ----- */
E("es-u4-418-q05",4,["EA12.4.18"],3,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("One earthquake was recorded at three seismograph stations. Station A recorded the P-waves at 10:02 and the S-waves at 10:06. Station B recorded the P-waves at 10:04 and the S-waves at 10:11. Station C recorded the P-waves at 10:01 and the S-waves at 10:03. Which statement about the stations' distances from the epicentre is correct?","Station C is the closest to the epicentre and Station B is the farthest.",["Station B is the closest to the epicentre and Station C is the farthest.","Station A is the closest to the epicentre and Station C is the farthest.","All three stations are about the same distance from the epicentre."],"Distance is read from the gap between the P and S arrivals at each station. The gaps are 4 minutes at Station A (10:06 minus 10:02), 7 minutes at Station B (10:11 minus 10:04) and 2 minutes at Station C (10:03 minus 10:01). S-waves fall further behind P-waves the longer both have travelled, so the smallest gap marks the shortest journey: Station C is closest, and Station B, with the largest gap, is farthest.",{});}]),["Work out the P-to-S gap at each station first; rank the gaps, not the clock times."]);

/* ----- EA12.9.5: Analyse examples of Canadian contributions to science ----- */
E("es-u4-95-q03",4,["EA12.9.5"],3,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Wilson's hot spot idea lets the ages of a volcanic island chain record plate motion. One island in a chain lies 300 km from the presently active volcano and has been dated at 6 million years old. At what average rate has the plate been moving over the hot spot?","5 cm per year",["50 cm per year","0.5 cm per year","180 cm per year"],"The island has been carried 300 km in 6 million years, so the plate averages 300 / 6 = 50 km per million years. Converting units: 50 km is 5,000,000 cm and one million years is 1,000,000 years, so the rate is 5,000,000 / 1,000,000 = 5 cm per year, a typical plate speed and the kind of estimate a hot spot track makes possible.",{});}]),["Divide distance by age first, then convert km per million years into cm per year."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.19 / EA12.4.20: Volcano types, eruption factors, and eruption type by boundary ----- */
E("es-u4-420-q04",4,["EA12.4.19","EA12.4.20"],3,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Two volcanoes are fed by different magmas. Volcano A is fed by magma with about 50% silica and about 1% dissolved gas. Volcano B is fed by magma with about 70% silica and about 5% dissolved gas. Which pairing of eruption style and volcano type is correct?","A: quiet, free-flowing lava flows that build a broad shield volcano; B: violent, explosive eruptions that build a steep ash and cinder cone",["A: violent, explosive eruptions that build a steep ash and cinder cone; B: quiet, free-flowing lava flows that build a broad shield volcano","A: quiet, free-flowing lava flows that build a broad shield volcano; B: alternating lava flows and explosive eruptions that build a composite cone","A: alternating lava flows and explosive eruptions that build a composite cone; B: violent, explosive eruptions that build a steep ash and cinder cone"],"Silica content sets the magma's viscosity, and dissolved gas supplies the explosive pressure. Volcano A's magma is the lowest in both silica and gas, so it is runny and releases its gas easily: it erupts in quiet, free-flowing flows that spread out into a broad shield volcano. Volcano B's magma is the highest in both silica and gas, so it is stiff and traps its gas until it bursts free: it erupts violently and explosively, piling fragments into a steep ash and cinder cone.",{});}]),["Rank each magma for silica and for gas, then match the lowest and the highest against the volcano table."]);

/* ----- EA12.4.2: Describe and give examples of the different types of convergent plate boundaries ----- */
E("es-u4-42-q09",4,["EA12.4.2"],3,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Along one plate boundary, earthquakes occur at shallow depths beneath an offshore trench, at intermediate depths beneath the coast, and at depths of several hundred kilometres far inland. Taken together, the earthquake foci define a single zone that dips inland at about 45 degrees. What does this pattern indicate?","Oceanic lithosphere is subducting beneath the overriding plate, and the earthquakes trace the descending slab.",["A transform fault is slipping sideways along the boundary, and the earthquakes deepen where the fault bends downward.","A mantle plume is rising beneath the continent, and the earthquakes trace the plume's path toward the surface.","Two continental plates are colliding head-on, and the earthquakes mark a fault zone that cuts vertically through the crust."],"Earthquake foci that deepen steadily along a dipping zone are the signature of a subducting slab: the cold oceanic lithosphere sinks into the mantle at about 45 degrees and keeps generating earthquakes as it descends, shallow near the trench and several hundred kilometres deep far inland. Transform faults produce shallow foci only, and a continental collision deforms the crust without sending a slab to such depths.",{});}]),["Ask what moving body could carry earthquake sources to several hundred kilometres deep along a sloping path."]);

